window.__TG1={"TG3A_I":"","TG3A_S":"","TG3B_I":"","TG3B_S":"","TG3C_I":"","TG3C_S":"","TG3D_I":"","TG3D_S":"","TG70B_I":"","TG70B_P":{"qr1":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part I: Question and Response (Items 1 to 10)<br>Directions: Choose the best response to each question or statement.","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Q:</b> Could you give me a hand with these boxes?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>R:</b> {{1}}</span>"],"qr2":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part I: Question and Response (Items 1 to 10)<br>Directions: Choose the best response to each question or statement.","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Q:</b> I heard you lost your part time job. I'm really sorry.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>R:</b> {{2}}</span>"],"qr3":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part I: Question and Response (Items 1 to 10)<br>Directions: Choose the best response to each question or statement.","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Q:</b> How often do you go to the gym?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>R:</b> {{3}}</span>"],"qr4":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part I: Question and Response (Items 1 to 10)<br>Directions: Choose the best response to each question or statement.","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Q:</b> Do you mind if I open the window?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>R:</b> {{4}}</span>"],"qr5":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part I: Question and Response (Items 1 to 10)<br>Directions: Choose the best response to each question or statement.","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Q:</b> What's the matter? You look upset.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>R:</b> {{5}}</span>"],"qr6":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part I: Question and Response (Items 1 to 10)<br>Directions: Choose the best response to each question or statement.","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Q:</b> I'm thinking of switching my major to economics.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>R:</b> {{6}}</span>"],"qr7":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part I: Question and Response (Items 1 to 10)<br>Directions: Choose the best response to each question or statement.","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Q:</b> Can I get you anything to drink?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>R:</b> {{7}}</span>"],"qr8":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part I: Question and Response (Items 1 to 10)<br>Directions: Choose the best response to each question or statement.","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Q:</b> How did your job interview go?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>R:</b> {{8}}</span>"],"qr9":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part I: Question and Response (Items 1 to 10)<br>Directions: Choose the best response to each question or statement.","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Q:</b> Let's grab some dinner after the movie.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>R:</b> {{9}}</span>"],"qr10":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part I: Question and Response (Items 1 to 10)<br>Directions: Choose the best response to each question or statement.","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Q:</b> Would you like me to save you a seat in the hall?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>R:</b> {{10}}</span>"],"c1":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part II: Short Conversations<br>Conversation 1 (Items 11 to 13)<br>Situation: At a train station ticket counter","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Passenger:</b> Excuse me. {{11}}?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Staff:</b> Yes, there are two. One leaves at 2:15 and the other at 4:40.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Passenger:</b> I'll take the 2:15, please. {{12}}?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Staff:</b> About four hours, so you'll arrive at around 6:15.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Passenger:</b> Okay. {{13}}?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Staff:</b> It's 300 baht for a second class seat.</span>"],"c2":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part II: Short Conversations<br>Conversation 2 (Items 14 to 16)<br>Situation: Two friends planning a weekend trip","<span class=\"xd\"><b>June:</b> Are we still going hiking on Saturday?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Beam:</b> {{14}}. The forecast says it's going to rain all day.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>June:</b> Oh no. {{15}}?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Beam:</b> Well, Sunday looks sunny, so we could go then.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>June:</b> Good idea. {{16}}.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Beam:</b> Great. I'll pick you up at seven, then.</span>"],"c3":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part II: Short Conversations<br>Conversation 3 (Items 17 to 20)<br>Situation: A phone call to a dental clinic","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Receptionist:</b> Bright Smile Dental Clinic. How can I help you?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Caller:</b> Hi, {{17}}.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Receptionist:</b> Of course. Have you visited us before?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Caller:</b> {{18}}</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Receptionist:</b> Then I should have your records. We have an opening on Tuesday at 3 p.m. {{19}}?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Caller:</b> I'm afraid I have class until four. Do you have anything later?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Receptionist:</b> How about 5:30?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Caller:</b> {{20}}</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Receptionist:</b> Great. See you on Tuesday, then.</span>"],"c4":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part III: Long Conversations<br>Conversation 1 (Items 21 to 25)<br>Situation: A student is interviewed for a summer internship at a museum","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Interviewer:</b> Thank you for coming in, Pim. {{21}}?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Pim:</b> I'm studying history, and I've always wanted to see how a museum works behind the scenes.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Interviewer:</b> Great. Our interns help with guided tours for visitors. {{22}}?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Pim:</b> Quite comfortable, actually. I'm the president of my school's debate club.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Interviewer:</b> Impressive. However, the internship sometimes involves weekend work. {{23}}?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Pim:</b> Not at all. I'm usually free on weekends.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Interviewer:</b> Good to hear. {{24}}?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Pim:</b> Yes. Will interns get any training before they lead tours?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Interviewer:</b> Absolutely. There is a training program that lasts two weeks. We will contact all applicants by Friday.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Pim:</b> {{25}}</span>"],"c5":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part III: Long Conversations<br>Conversation 2 (Items 26 to 30)<br>Situation: Two roommates talking about housework","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Ken:</b> Hey, Tom. Do you have a minute? {{26}}.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Tom:</b> Sure. What's up?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Ken:</b> Well, the dishes keep piling up in the sink. {{27}}.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Tom:</b> Oh, I'm sorry. I've been so busy with exams that I didn't even notice.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Ken:</b> I understand. {{28}}?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Tom:</b> That's a good idea. How about I do the dishes on weekdays and you do them on weekends?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Ken:</b> {{29}}?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Tom:</b> I'll take it out every Monday and Thursday.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Ken:</b> Perfect. {{30}}.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Tom:</b> No problem. Thanks for telling me instead of getting angry.</span>"],"r1":["SECTION II: READING SKILL (Items 31 to 60)<br>Part I: Text Completion<br>Passage 1 (Items 31 to 35)<br>Directions: Choose the best answer to complete the passage.","<span class=\"xp\">Bangkok was once called the “Venice of the East” because of its huge network of canals, known in Thai as <b>khlong</b>. For centuries, these waterways {{31}} as the main routes for transport and trade, and floating markets were a common sight along their banks.</span><span class=\"xp\">As more roads {{32}} in the twentieth century, many canals were filled in to make room for cars. The canals that remained often became dumping grounds for waste, and the water turned dark and smelly. {{33}}, many residents began to see the canals as a problem rather than a treasure.</span><span class=\"xp\">Today, the city is trying to bring its waterways back to life. Canal boat services help commuters {{34}} the heavy traffic on the roads, and some neighborhoods have turned their canal banks into walking paths and small parks. However, cleaning the water remains a challenge {{35}} wastewater from homes and businesses still flows directly into many canals.</span>"],"r2":["SECTION II: READING SKILL (Items 31 to 60)<br>Part I: Text Completion<br>Passage 2 (Items 36 to 40)<br>Directions: Choose the best answer to complete the passage.","<span class=\"xp\">Many parents complain that their teenage children stay up late at night and find it almost impossible to wake up in the morning. While this is often blamed on laziness, scientists say that biology {{36}} a major role.</span><span class=\"xp\">During adolescence, the body begins to release melatonin, the hormone that makes us feel sleepy, about two hours later than it does in children and adults. As a result, most teenagers do not feel tired until close to midnight. Experts recommend {{37}} eight to ten hours of sleep a night, but early school start times make this goal difficult to reach.</span><span class=\"xp\">Students who sleep too little are more likely to have trouble concentrating, and they are also {{38}} to accidents and mood problems. Some schools in other countries have moved their start times later, and studies show that their students now get more sleep and pay more attention in class. If more schools {{39}} the same, teenagers might perform better without studying any harder. In the meantime, experts advise teenagers to {{40}} screens at least an hour before bedtime, because the light from phones can delay sleep even further.</span>"],"r3":["SECTION II: READING SKILL (Items 31 to 60)<br>Part I: Text Completion<br>Passage 3 (Items 41 to 45)<br>Directions: Choose the best answer to complete the passage.","<span class=\"xp\">Not long ago, playing video games was seen as a waste of time. Today, competitive gaming, or esports, has grown into a global industry {{41}} billions of dollars. Professional players train for many hours a day, follow strict diets, and work with coaches, much like traditional athletes.</span><span class=\"xp\">The popularity of esports has also changed how people view gaming. Major tournaments now fill large stadiums, and millions more watch online. Esports was {{42}} as a medal event at the Asian Games in 2022, and many universities now offer scholarships to talented players.</span><span class=\"xp\">Despite this success, critics {{43}} concerns about the health of young players. Sitting in front of a screen for long periods can lead to eye strain, back pain, and poor sleep. Some also worry that young people may give up their studies in the hope of becoming professionals, {{44}} only a tiny number ever earn a living from gaming. Supporters reply that, like any sport, esports can teach teamwork and discipline, {{45}} it is practiced in a balanced way.</span>"],"p1":["SECTION II: READING SKILL (Items 31 to 60)<br>Part II: Reading Comprehension<br>Passage 1 (Items 46 to 50)<br>Directions: Read the passage and choose the best answer to each question.","<span class=\"xp\">(1) Every Saturday morning, a small wooden house near the beach in a fishing village in Trang fills with children. Some sit on mats reading picture books, while others gather around a volunteer who is reading aloud. The house is a community library, and it exists because of a woman named Nok, who is only 24 years old.</span><span class=\"xp\">(2) Nok grew up in the village, where most families depend on fishing. As a child, she rarely had books of her own, and the nearest public library was more than an hour away by bus. After finishing university, she returned home and noticed that little had changed. Many children could read only slowly, and few of them read for pleasure.</span><span class=\"xp\">(3) With her savings, Nok repaired her late grandfather’s old house and filled its shelves with books donated by friends and online followers. At first, parents were unsure. They worried that reading would distract their children from helping with the family business. Nok invited them to a reading event, where they watched their children perform short plays based on stories they had read. Several parents were so moved that they offered to help run the library.</span><span class=\"xp\">(4) Three years later, the library has more than 3,000 books and a team of volunteers. Teachers at the local school say that students’ reading scores have improved. For Nok, however, the greatest reward is simpler. “When a child asks to borrow a second book before finishing the first,” she says, “I know we are doing something right.”</span>"],"p2":["SECTION II: READING SKILL (Items 31 to 60)<br>Part II: Reading Comprehension<br>Passage 2 (Items 51 to 55)<br>Directions: Read the passage and choose the best answer to each question.","<span class=\"xp\">(1) Almost everyone has put off an important task at some point, whether it is studying for an exam or answering a difficult email. This habit, known as procrastination, is often seen as a sign of laziness or poor time management. However, psychologists now believe that it has more to do with emotions than with time.</span><span class=\"xp\">(2) According to this view, people delay tasks that make them feel bad, such as bored, anxious, or afraid of failing. By turning to something more pleasant, like scrolling through social media, they get a quick sense of relief. Unfortunately, this relief does not last. The task is still waiting, and the stress grows stronger as the deadline approaches.</span><span class=\"xp\">(3) This explains why simply making a detailed schedule often fails to cure procrastination. If the underlying feelings are not addressed, people will continue to avoid the task no matter how well they plan their time. Researchers suggest more effective strategies, such as breaking a large task into very small steps and focusing only on the first one. Another approach is to treat oneself with kindness after procrastinating. Studies have found that students who forgave themselves for delaying their studies were less likely to procrastinate before the next exam.</span><span class=\"xp\">(4) In short, beating procrastination may depend less on discipline and more on understanding our own feelings. The next time you find yourself avoiding a task, it may help to ask not “Why am I so lazy?” but “What about this task makes me uncomfortable?”</span>"],"p3":["SECTION II: READING SKILL (Items 31 to 60)<br>Part II: Reading Comprehension<br>Passage 3 (Items 56 to 60)<br>Directions: Read the passage and choose the best answer to each question.","<span class=\"xp\">(1) In many Western countries, it is common for students to take a gap year, a break of about a year between finishing high school and starting university. In Thailand, however, the idea is still unusual. Most students move directly from one stage of education to the next, and a year away from school is often viewed as falling behind.</span><span class=\"xp\">(2) Supporters of the gap year argue that it gives young people valuable time to discover who they are. Instead of choosing a university major under pressure, students can work, travel, volunteer, or try out different fields before making a decision. Many return with greater maturity and a clearer sense of purpose. Some universities abroad even encourage accepted students to delay their entry for a year.</span><span class=\"xp\">(3) Critics, however, point out that a gap year is not equally available to everyone. Traveling abroad or doing unpaid volunteer work can be expensive, so the experience may mostly benefit students from wealthy families. There is also the risk that a year without clear goals becomes a year wasted. Some students find it hard to return to the routine of studying after a long break.</span><span class=\"xp\">(4) The value of a gap year, then, seems to depend largely on how it is spent. A year that is carefully planned can be as educational as any classroom, while an unplanned one may simply delay the future. For students considering this option, the key question is not whether to take a break, but what they hope to gain from it.</span>"]},"TG70C_I":"","TG70C_P":{"qr1":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part I: Question and Response (Items 1 to 10)<br>Directions: Choose the best response to each question or statement.","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Q:</b> I love your new haircut! It really suits you.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>R:</b> {{1}}</span>"],"qr2":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part I: Question and Response (Items 1 to 10)<br>Directions: Choose the best response to each question or statement.","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Q:</b> Excuse me, how do I get to the nearest BTS station?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>R:</b> {{2}}</span>"],"qr3":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part I: Question and Response (Items 1 to 10)<br>Directions: Choose the best response to each question or statement.","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Q:</b> I don't really like horror movies.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>R:</b> {{3}}</span>"],"qr4":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part I: Question and Response (Items 1 to 10)<br>Directions: Choose the best response to each question or statement.","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Q:</b> Sorry, could you say that again? It's a bit noisy in here.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>R:</b> {{4}}</span>"],"qr5":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part I: Question and Response (Items 1 to 10)<br>Directions: Choose the best response to each question or statement.","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Q:</b> Shall I help you carry those books to the library?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>R:</b> {{5}}</span>"],"qr6":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part I: Question and Response (Items 1 to 10)<br>Directions: Choose the best response to each question or statement.","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Q:</b> I wish I had studied harder for the test.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>R:</b> {{6}}</span>"],"qr7":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part I: Question and Response (Items 1 to 10)<br>Directions: Choose the best response to each question or statement.","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Q:</b> This soup is far too salty. Could I speak to the manager?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>R:</b> {{7}}</span>"],"qr8":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part I: Question and Response (Items 1 to 10)<br>Directions: Choose the best response to each question or statement.","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Q:</b> May I borrow your charger for a few minutes?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>R:</b> {{8}}</span>"],"qr9":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part I: Question and Response (Items 1 to 10)<br>Directions: Choose the best response to each question or statement.","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Q:</b> You've been working here for ten years? That's amazing!</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>R:</b> {{9}}</span>"],"qr10":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part I: Question and Response (Items 1 to 10)<br>Directions: Choose the best response to each question or statement.","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Q:</b> It was great seeing you again. Let's keep in touch!</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>R:</b> {{10}}</span>"],"c1":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part II: Short Conversations<br>Conversation 1 (Items 11 to 13)<br>Situation: At a restaurant","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Waiter:</b> Good evening. {{11}}?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Customer:</b> No, we don't. Is there a table for two available?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Waiter:</b> Yes, right this way, please. Here are your menus. {{12}}?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Customer:</b> Two iced teas, please. And {{13}}? My friend is vegetarian.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Waiter:</b> Yes. Our green curry can be made with tofu instead of chicken.</span>"],"c2":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part II: Short Conversations<br>Conversation 2 (Items 14 to 16)<br>Situation: Two classmates talking on the phone","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Fah:</b> Hey, Win. Did I miss anything important in class today?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Win:</b> {{14}}. The teacher announced a quiz for next Monday.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Fah:</b> Oh no! {{15}}?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Win:</b> Chapters three and four. I'll send you my notes tonight.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Fah:</b> {{16}}</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Win:</b> No problem. That's what friends are for.</span>"],"c3":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part II: Short Conversations<br>Conversation 3 (Items 17 to 20)<br>Situation: At the front desk of a fitness center","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Staff:</b> Hi there. Welcome to FitZone.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Customer:</b> Hi. {{17}}.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Staff:</b> Great. We have two plans. The monthly plan is 1,200 baht, and the yearly plan is 10,800 baht.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Customer:</b> {{18}}?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Staff:</b> Yes, it does. Yearly members get free yoga and swimming classes.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Customer:</b> That sounds good. {{19}}?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Staff:</b> Of course. We offer a free trial for one day. You just need to show your ID card.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Customer:</b> {{20}}</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Staff:</b> See you then!</span>"],"c4":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part III: Long Conversations<br>Conversation 1 (Items 21 to 25)<br>Situation: A university student visits a dormitory near campus","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Owner:</b> Hello. {{21}}?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Student:</b> Yes, I saw your post online. Is it still available?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Owner:</b> It is. It's on the third floor, and it has a private bathroom and a small balcony.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Student:</b> Sounds nice. {{22}}?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Owner:</b> It's 5,500 baht a month, not including electricity and water.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Student:</b> I see. {{23}}?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Owner:</b> Yes. Two months' rent in advance, which you'll get back when you move out.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Student:</b> That's fine. What about the rules? {{24}}?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Owner:</b> I'm afraid not. For security reasons, all visitors must leave by ten at night.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Student:</b> Understood. I think I'd like to take the room.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Owner:</b> {{25}}.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Student:</b> Sure. Do I need to bring anything?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Owner:</b> Just your ID card and the deposit.</span>"],"c5":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part III: Long Conversations<br>Conversation 2 (Items 26 to 30)<br>Situation: A coordinator talks to volunteers before a beach cleanup","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Coordinator:</b> Good morning, everyone, and thank you for coming. {{26}}.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Volunteer:</b> How long will the cleanup take?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Coordinator:</b> About three hours. We'll work in teams of five along the beach.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Volunteer:</b> {{27}}?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Coordinator:</b> Put them in the blue bags. They will be sent to a recycling center.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Volunteer:</b> And {{28}}?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Coordinator:</b> Don't touch it with your hands. Use the tongs and put it in the red box.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Volunteer:</b> {{29}}?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Coordinator:</b> We have gloves and hats for everyone at the tent over there.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Coordinator:</b> One more thing. {{30}}.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Volunteer:</b> Thanks for the reminder. Let's get started!</span>"],"r1":["SECTION II: READING SKILL (Items 31 to 60)<br>Part I: Text Completion<br>Passage 1 (Items 31 to 35)<br>Directions: Choose the best answer to complete the passage.","<span class=\"xp\">In 2019, traditional Thai massage, known as <b>nuad thai</b>, was added to the UNESCO list of the Intangible Cultural Heritage of Humanity. The practice, which {{31}} down from teachers to students for generations, is now recognized as an important part of Thai culture.</span><span class=\"xp\">Unlike many types of massage that mainly aim to relax the muscles, nuad thai is {{32}} on the belief that energy flows through the body along invisible lines. Practitioners use their hands, elbows, knees, and even feet to apply pressure {{33}} specific points along these lines, and they gently stretch the body in ways similar to yoga.</span><span class=\"xp\">{{34}} it was once taught mainly in temples, such as Wat Pho in Bangkok, nuad thai is now taught at schools around the world. Supporters hope that the UNESCO recognition will help {{35}} this traditional knowledge for future generations.</span>"],"r2":["SECTION II: READING SKILL (Items 31 to 60)<br>Part I: Text Completion<br>Passage 2 (Items 36 to 40)<br>Directions: Choose the best answer to complete the passage.","<span class=\"xp\">Chiang Mai has become one of the most popular cities in the world for digital nomads, people who work online while traveling from place to place. The city {{36}} them with its low cost of living, reliable internet, and relaxed lifestyle.</span><span class=\"xp\">In recent years, cafes and shared offices {{37}} all over the city to serve this growing community. Many local business owners welcome the extra income. {{38}}, some residents worry that the rising demand for housing has pushed up rents, making it harder for local families to find homes they can afford.</span><span class=\"xp\">{{39}} attract more remote workers, Thailand has introduced a new type of visa that allows them to stay in the country for longer periods. The challenge now is to build a city that welcomes newcomers while remaining {{40}} for the people who have always lived there.</span>"],"r3":["SECTION II: READING SKILL (Items 31 to 60)<br>Part I: Text Completion<br>Passage 3 (Items 41 to 45)<br>Directions: Choose the best answer to complete the passage.","<span class=\"xp\">Fast fashion refers to cheap, trendy clothing that is designed, produced, and sold very quickly. It allows shoppers to {{41}} with the latest styles without spending much money. However, many of these clothes are worn only a few times {{42}} being thrown away.</span><span class=\"xp\">The environmental cost of this habit is high. Growing cotton and dyeing fabric require {{43}} water, and much of the dye ends up in rivers. In addition, many cheap garments are made from synthetic materials that release tiny pieces of plastic when they are washed.</span><span class=\"xp\">{{44}} more consumers become aware of these problems, some brands have started to offer repair services and to sell secondhand clothing. Experts say that buying less and choosing better quality can make a real difference. In fact, they often point out that the most sustainable piece of clothing is the one {{45}} you already own.</span>"],"p1":["SECTION II: READING SKILL (Items 31 to 60)<br>Part II: Reading Comprehension<br>Passage 1 (Items 46 to 50)<br>Directions: Read the passage and choose the best answer to each question.","<span class=\"xp\">(1) For two years, Arm spent his days delivering food around Bangkok on his motorcycle. At red lights, while other riders checked their phones for new orders, he took out a small notebook of English vocabulary and quietly tested himself.</span><span class=\"xp\">(2) Arm had left school at sixteen when his father fell ill and the family needed money. Although he had been a good student, he believed that university was no longer a realistic goal for someone like him. That changed when a regular customer, a retired teacher, noticed his notebook and asked what he was studying. When she heard his story, she offered to tutor him for free every Sunday.</span><span class=\"xp\">(3) Studying while working full time was exhausting. Arm often slept only five hours a night, and there were times when he wanted to give up. His tutor kept reminding him that progress did not have to be fast, only steady. Little by little, his scores on practice tests improved.</span><span class=\"xp\">(4) Last year, at the age of 22, Arm was accepted into a public university to study engineering. He still delivers food on weekends to support his family, but these days he carries textbooks instead of a small notebook. “I used to think my life was already decided,” he says. “Now I know it can still be changed.”</span>"],"p2":["SECTION II: READING SKILL (Items 31 to 60)<br>Part II: Reading Comprehension<br>Passage 2 (Items 51 to 55)<br>Directions: Read the passage and choose the best answer to each question.","<span class=\"xp\">(1) Bees are among the most important animals on Earth, although many people only think about them when they are afraid of being stung. Around three quarters of the world’s major food crops depend at least partly on pollinators such as bees. Without them, foods like apples, almonds, and coffee would become much scarcer and more expensive.</span><span class=\"xp\">(2) In recent decades, however, bee populations in many parts of the world have declined. Scientists point to several causes. Some pesticides used on farms can damage bees’ nervous systems, making it harder for them to find their way back to their hives. The loss of wild flowers, as land is turned into farms and cities, also leaves bees with less food. Climate change adds further pressure by changing the time of year when flowers bloom.</span><span class=\"xp\">(3) Fortunately, some solutions are surprisingly simple. Farmers can plant strips of wild flowers along the edges of their fields, and city residents can grow flowering plants on balconies and rooftops. Several countries have also restricted the use of certain pesticides that are known to harm bees.</span><span class=\"xp\">(4) Protecting bees, scientists argue, is not only about saving one type of insect. It is about protecting the food we eat and the natural systems that support all life on the planet.</span>"],"p3":["SECTION II: READING SKILL (Items 31 to 60)<br>Part II: Reading Comprehension<br>Passage 3 (Items 56 to 60)<br>Directions: Read the passage and choose the best answer to each question.","<span class=\"xp\">(1) Every year, hundreds of thousands of Thai students sit for university entrance exams. For many of them, a few days of testing will shape the next four years of their lives. This has led to an ongoing debate: should a single set of test scores decide a student’s future?</span><span class=\"xp\">(2) Supporters of exams argue that they are the fairest method available. Everyone answers the same questions under the same conditions, and the results cannot easily be influenced by money or personal connections. Exams also give universities a quick and consistent way to compare large numbers of applicants.</span><span class=\"xp\">(3) Critics, however, say that exams measure only a narrow range of abilities. A student who is a talented musician, a skilled debater, or a respected community leader may not be able to show those strengths on a multiple choice test. They also point out that students from wealthy families can pay for expensive tutoring schools, so the competition is not as equal as it seems.</span><span class=\"xp\">(4) In response, many universities now combine test scores with other methods, such as portfolios and interviews. As one admissions officer puts it, “The question is not whether exams are good or bad, but whether they are enough on their own.”</span>"]},"TG70D_I":"","TG70D_P":{"qr1":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part I: Question and Response (Items 1 to 10)<br>Directions: Choose the best response to each question or statement.","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Q:</b> Congratulations on winning the speech contest!</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>R:</b> {{1}}</span>"],"qr2":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part I: Question and Response (Items 1 to 10)<br>Directions: Choose the best response to each question or statement.","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Q:</b> Would you rather cook at home or order food tonight?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>R:</b> {{2}}</span>"],"qr3":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part I: Question and Response (Items 1 to 10)<br>Directions: Choose the best response to each question or statement.","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Q:</b> How come you're still awake? It's past midnight.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>R:</b> {{3}}</span>"],"qr4":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part I: Question and Response (Items 1 to 10)<br>Directions: Choose the best response to each question or statement.","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Q:</b> Could you keep it down a little? I'm trying to study.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>R:</b> {{4}}</span>"],"qr5":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part I: Question and Response (Items 1 to 10)<br>Directions: Choose the best response to each question or statement.","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Q:</b> Which bus goes to Central World?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>R:</b> {{5}}</span>"],"qr6":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part I: Question and Response (Items 1 to 10)<br>Directions: Choose the best response to each question or statement.","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Q:</b> I'm afraid I can't make it to your party on Saturday.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>R:</b> {{6}}</span>"],"qr7":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part I: Question and Response (Items 1 to 10)<br>Directions: Choose the best response to each question or statement.","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Q:</b> Do you know where I can get my phone repaired?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>R:</b> {{7}}</span>"],"qr8":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part I: Question and Response (Items 1 to 10)<br>Directions: Choose the best response to each question or statement.","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Q:</b> What would you do if you won the lottery?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>R:</b> {{8}}</span>"],"qr9":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part I: Question and Response (Items 1 to 10)<br>Directions: Choose the best response to each question or statement.","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Q:</b> Thanks so much for lending me your notes.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>R:</b> {{9}}</span>"],"qr10":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part I: Question and Response (Items 1 to 10)<br>Directions: Choose the best response to each question or statement.","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Q:</b> Excuse me, is this seat taken?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>R:</b> {{10}}</span>"],"c1":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part II: Short Conversations<br>Conversation 1 (Items 11 to 13)<br>Situation: At a post office","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Clerk:</b> Next, please.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Customer:</b> Hi. {{11}}.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Clerk:</b> Sure. Would you like regular or express delivery?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Customer:</b> {{12}}?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Clerk:</b> Regular is 45 baht and takes three days. Express is 90 baht and arrives tomorrow.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Customer:</b> I'll take express, then. {{13}}?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Clerk:</b> Yes. Your tracking number is printed on this receipt.</span>"],"c2":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part II: Short Conversations<br>Conversation 2 (Items 14 to 16)<br>Situation: Two coworkers at an office","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Nan:</b> The meeting has been moved to 3 p.m. Did you get the email?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Joe:</b> {{14}}. I've been out of the office all morning.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Nan:</b> Well, {{15}}.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Joe:</b> Oh no, I haven't finished mine yet. I'd better start now.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Nan:</b> {{16}}?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Joe:</b> That would be great. Could you check the numbers for me?</span>"],"c3":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part II: Short Conversations<br>Conversation 3 (Items 17 to 20)<br>Situation: At the lost and found counter of a shopping mall","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Customer:</b> Excuse me. {{17}}.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Staff:</b> I see. Can you describe it?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Customer:</b> {{18}}</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Staff:</b> Let me check. Is this yours?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Customer:</b> Yes, that's it! {{19}}?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Staff:</b> One of our cleaners brought it in about twenty minutes ago.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Customer:</b> {{20}}</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Staff:</b> I will. Please sign this form before you go.</span>"],"c4":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part III: Long Conversations<br>Conversation 1 (Items 21 to 25)<br>Situation: A high school student meets a guidance counselor","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Counselor:</b> Hi, Mew. Come and sit down. {{21}}?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Mew:</b> Not yet. I'm stuck between psychology and social work.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Counselor:</b> Both are great choices. {{22}}?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Mew:</b> I like learning how the mind works, but I also want to help people in the community directly.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Counselor:</b> In that case, {{23}}.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Mew:</b> That's a good idea. Do you know anyone I could contact?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Counselor:</b> Yes. {{24}}.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Mew:</b> Really? I'd love to ask her about her job.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Counselor:</b> I'll send you her email address. {{25}}.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Mew:</b> Of course. Thank you so much for your help.</span>"],"c5":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part III: Long Conversations<br>Conversation 2 (Items 26 to 30)<br>Situation: Two friends planning a surprise for a classmate","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Pond:</b> Hey, Ice. Joy's birthday is next Friday. {{26}}?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Ice:</b> Great idea! Where should we have it?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Pond:</b> {{27}}.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Ice:</b> Perfect. How many people should we invite?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Pond:</b> {{28}}.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Ice:</b> Okay. I'll take care of the cake. {{29}}?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Pond:</b> Sure. I'll buy some balloons and string lights.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Ice:</b> Great. {{30}}.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Pond:</b> Don't worry. My lips are sealed.</span>"],"r1":["SECTION II: READING SKILL (Items 31 to 60)<br>Part I: Text Completion<br>Passage 1 (Items 31 to 35)<br>Directions: Choose the best answer to complete the passage.","<span class=\"xp\">Thailand is the largest exporter of durian in the world. The durian is a fruit {{31}} smell is so strong that it is banned in many hotels and on public transport in some countries. Yet for its many fans, the creamy flesh is worth every bit of the smell.</span><span class=\"xp\">Demand from Chinese consumers, who see durian as a luxury gift, {{32}} grown rapidly in recent years. To meet this demand, durian farms have {{33}} across eastern and southern Thailand, and many farmers have switched from other crops to durian.</span><span class=\"xp\">However, the boom has also brought problems. Some sellers have {{34}} durians before they were ripe in order to sell them earlier at higher prices, which has damaged buyers’ trust. {{35}} protect the reputation of Thai durian, the authorities now inspect fruit more carefully before it is exported.</span>"],"r2":["SECTION II: READING SKILL (Items 31 to 60)<br>Part I: Text Completion<br>Passage 2 (Items 36 to 40)<br>Directions: Choose the best answer to complete the passage.","<span class=\"xp\">Many people believe that children learn languages more {{36}} than adults. It is true that young children often achieve better pronunciation. However, adults have advantages of their own. They can use their knowledge of grammar and their study skills to learn {{37}} than children in the early stages.</span><span class=\"xp\">Learning a new language may also {{38}} the brain. Some studies suggest that people who speak two languages are better at switching between tasks, and that using more than one language may help delay memory problems in old age.</span><span class=\"xp\">Whatever your age, the key is regular practice. The {{39}} you use a language, the more confident you become. So if you have always dreamed of speaking another language, remember that it is never too late {{40}} one.</span>"],"r3":["SECTION II: READING SKILL (Items 31 to 60)<br>Part I: Text Completion<br>Passage 3 (Items 41 to 45)<br>Directions: Choose the best answer to complete the passage.","<span class=\"xp\">Online scams have become one of the most common crimes in Thailand. Criminals often {{41}} to be police officers or bank staff in order to gain their victims’ trust. They create a sense of urgency, telling victims that their bank accounts will be frozen {{42}} they transfer money immediately.</span><span class=\"xp\">Other scammers send fake links that lead to websites almost identical {{43}} real ones. When victims enter their personal information, the criminals use it to steal money from their accounts.</span><span class=\"xp\">Although older people are often targeted, young people are by no means {{44}}. Experts advise {{45}} passwords or verification codes with anyone, even someone who claims to work for a bank. If in doubt, hang up and call the bank directly using the number on its official website.</span>"],"p1":["SECTION II: READING SKILL (Items 31 to 60)<br>Part II: Reading Comprehension<br>Passage 1 (Items 46 to 50)<br>Directions: Read the passage and choose the best answer to each question.","<span class=\"xp\">(1) When customers first walk into Mai’s small cafe in Chiang Mai, they are often surprised. There is no music, and the staff do not take orders by speaking. Instead, customers point to pictures on the menu or write their orders on small cards, and the baristas reply with smiles and simple hand signs.</span><span class=\"xp\">(2) Mai, who was born deaf, opened the cafe three years ago after struggling for a long time to find work. “Employers would read my resume and seem interested,” she explains through an interpreter, “but once they found out I couldn’t hear, the interviews usually ended quickly.”</span><span class=\"xp\">(3) Determined to create opportunities for people like herself, Mai hired five deaf staff members and trained them as baristas. She also offers free sign language lessons to customers every Saturday morning. The lessons have become so popular that there is now a waiting list.</span><span class=\"xp\">(4) For Mai, the cafe is about more than coffee. “Many hearing people have never talked with a deaf person,” she says. “Here, they learn that communication is not only about sound. It is about the willingness to understand each other.”</span>"],"p2":["SECTION II: READING SKILL (Items 31 to 60)<br>Part II: Reading Comprehension<br>Passage 2 (Items 51 to 55)<br>Directions: Read the passage and choose the best answer to each question.","<span class=\"xp\">(1) Along the coasts of southern Thailand, mangrove forests grow where the land meets the sea. Their tangled roots, which rise above the water like the legs of a table, may look strange, but they make mangroves one of the most valuable ecosystems on Earth.</span><span class=\"xp\">(2) Mangroves protect coastal communities by acting as natural barriers. Their roots slow down waves and reduce the damage caused by storms. After the Indian Ocean tsunami in 2004, researchers reported that some villages located behind healthy mangrove forests suffered less damage than villages without them.</span><span class=\"xp\">(3) Mangroves also serve as nurseries for marine life. Many kinds of fish, crabs, and shrimp lay their eggs among the roots, where the young are protected from larger predators. In addition, mangroves store large amounts of carbon in their muddy soil, which helps to slow down climate change.</span><span class=\"xp\">(4) Despite these benefits, large areas of mangrove forest were cleared in past decades to make way for shrimp farms and tourist resorts. Today, many local communities are working with the government to replant mangroves along the coast. Their efforts show that protecting nature and protecting people often go hand in hand.</span>"],"p3":["SECTION II: READING SKILL (Items 31 to 60)<br>Part II: Reading Comprehension<br>Passage 3 (Items 56 to 60)<br>Directions: Read the passage and choose the best answer to each question.","<span class=\"xp\">(1) In Thailand, students in school uniforms are a familiar sight every weekday morning. Recently, however, some schools have started allowing students to wear their own clothes on certain days, and this has reopened an old debate about whether uniforms are still necessary.</span><span class=\"xp\">(2) Supporters believe that uniforms create a sense of equality. When everyone dresses the same, students from poorer families are less likely to be judged by what they wear. Uniforms also save time in the morning and make it easy for teachers to identify their students on school trips.</span><span class=\"xp\">(3) Opponents argue that uniforms limit students’ freedom to express themselves. They also point out that buying several sets of uniforms each year can be expensive, which ironically puts pressure on the very families that uniforms are supposed to help. Moreover, some studies have found no clear link between wearing uniforms and better behavior or higher grades.</span><span class=\"xp\">(4) Several schools have chosen a middle path: students wear uniforms on most days but may dress freely once a week. As one school director explains, “The point is not the clothes themselves, but what they teach students about belonging and responsibility.”</span>"]},"TG70E_I":"","TG70E_P":{"qr1":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part I: Question and Response (Items 1 to 10)<br>Directions: Choose the best response to each question or statement.","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Q:</b> Could you take a photo of us in front of the temple?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>R:</b> {{1}}</span>"],"qr2":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part I: Question and Response (Items 1 to 10)<br>Directions: Choose the best response to each question or statement.","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Q:</b> I'm sorry I'm late. The traffic was terrible.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>R:</b> {{2}}</span>"],"qr3":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part I: Question and Response (Items 1 to 10)<br>Directions: Choose the best response to each question or statement.","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Q:</b> What's the weather like in Chiang Mai in December?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>R:</b> {{3}}</span>"],"qr4":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part I: Question and Response (Items 1 to 10)<br>Directions: Choose the best response to each question or statement.","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Q:</b> Have you ever tried skydiving?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>R:</b> {{4}}</span>"],"qr5":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part I: Question and Response (Items 1 to 10)<br>Directions: Choose the best response to each question or statement.","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Q:</b> Do you think it will rain this afternoon?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>R:</b> {{5}}</span>"],"qr6":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part I: Question and Response (Items 1 to 10)<br>Directions: Choose the best response to each question or statement.","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Q:</b> Could you tell me what time the museum closes?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>R:</b> {{6}}</span>"],"qr7":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part I: Question and Response (Items 1 to 10)<br>Directions: Choose the best response to each question or statement.","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Q:</b> I've got a terrible headache.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>R:</b> {{7}}</span>"],"qr8":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part I: Question and Response (Items 1 to 10)<br>Directions: Choose the best response to each question or statement.","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Q:</b> Mind your step! The floor is still wet.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>R:</b> {{8}}</span>"],"qr9":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part I: Question and Response (Items 1 to 10)<br>Directions: Choose the best response to each question or statement.","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Q:</b> Who's in charge of the school festival this year?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>R:</b> {{9}}</span>"],"qr10":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part I: Question and Response (Items 1 to 10)<br>Directions: Choose the best response to each question or statement.","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Q:</b> Can you believe it's already November?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>R:</b> {{10}}</span>"],"c1":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part II: Short Conversations<br>Conversation 1 (Items 11 to 13)<br>Situation: At a cinema ticket counter","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Staff:</b> Hi there. {{11}}?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Customer:</b> Two tickets for Moonlight Island at 7:30, please.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Staff:</b> I'm sorry, but the 7:30 show is sold out. {{12}}?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Customer:</b> Hmm, that's a bit late, but it's fine.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Staff:</b> Where would you like to sit?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Customer:</b> {{13}}.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Staff:</b> No problem. Row G, seats 10 and 11.</span>"],"c2":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part II: Short Conversations<br>Conversation 2 (Items 14 to 16)<br>Situation: Two neighbors talking outside their houses","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Mr. Lee:</b> Excuse me, is this your car? {{14}}.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Ms. Dao:</b> Oh, I'm so sorry! I didn't realize.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Mr. Lee:</b> {{15}}.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Ms. Dao:</b> Of course. I'll move it right away. {{16}}.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Mr. Lee:</b> Thanks. I really appreciate it.</span>"],"c3":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part II: Short Conversations<br>Conversation 3 (Items 17 to 20)<br>Situation: The beginning of an online class","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Teacher:</b> Good morning, everyone. Kai, {{17}}?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Kai:</b> Yes, I can, but your video keeps freezing.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Teacher:</b> Hmm. {{18}}.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Kai:</b> That's much better now. Your voice is clearer, too.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Teacher:</b> Great. Before we start, {{19}}?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Kai:</b> I haven't yet. Where can I find it?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Teacher:</b> {{20}}.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Kai:</b> Got it. Thank you.</span>"],"c4":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part III: Long Conversations<br>Conversation 1 (Items 21 to 25)<br>Situation: A student volunteers at a care home for older people","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Staff:</b> Welcome to Sunshine Home. {{21}}?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Volunteer:</b> Yes, it is. To be honest, I'm a little nervous.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Staff:</b> Don't worry. {{22}}.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Volunteer:</b> That sounds nice. What kind of things do they like to talk about?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Staff:</b> {{23}}.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Volunteer:</b> I'd love to hear those.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Staff:</b> Just one thing. {{24}}.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Volunteer:</b> I'll keep that in mind.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Staff:</b> {{25}}?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Volunteer:</b> Yes, please. I'm ready.</span>"],"c5":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part III: Long Conversations<br>Conversation 2 (Items 26 to 30)<br>Situation: Two friends planning a trip on a budget","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Bas:</b> I've been checking prices for our trip to Krabi. {{26}}.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Kim:</b> Really? How much more?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Bas:</b> {{27}}.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Kim:</b> That's a lot. Is there any way to save money?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Bas:</b> {{28}}?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Kim:</b> Good idea, but the bus takes twelve hours.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Bas:</b> True. {{29}}?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Kim:</b> That works for me. Guesthouses are usually cheaper anyway.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Bas:</b> {{30}}.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Kim:</b> Great. Send me the link when you find a good one.</span>"],"r1":["SECTION II: READING SKILL (Items 31 to 60)<br>Part I: Text Completion<br>Passage 1 (Items 31 to 35)<br>Directions: Choose the best answer to complete the passage.","<span class=\"xp\">Bangkok is famous around the world for its street food. From grilled pork on sticks to spicy papaya salad, cheap and delicious meals can be found on almost every corner. In 2018, a street food cook known as Jay Fai became the {{31}} street vendor in Bangkok to receive a Michelin star.</span><span class=\"xp\">The award {{32}} international attention to the city’s street food culture. Since then, long lines have formed outside her small shop, with some customers waiting {{33}} hours to taste her famous crab omelet.</span><span class=\"xp\">However, the city has also tried to remove vendors from some busy streets {{34}} reduce traffic and improve cleanliness. Many people fear that without careful planning, Bangkok could lose an important part of its {{35}}.</span>"],"r2":["SECTION II: READING SKILL (Items 31 to 60)<br>Part I: Text Completion<br>Passage 2 (Items 36 to 40)<br>Directions: Choose the best answer to complete the passage.","<span class=\"xp\">Walking is {{36}} forms of exercise. It requires no special equipment, and almost anyone can do it. Unlike running, it puts little stress on the joints, {{37}} it suitable for people of all ages.</span><span class=\"xp\">Research shows that walking for just thirty minutes a day can lower the risk of heart disease and improve mood. People who walk regularly are also less likely {{38}} from depression.</span><span class=\"xp\">{{39}} these benefits, many people find it hard to fit walking into their busy schedules. Experts suggest making small changes, such as taking the stairs instead of the elevator or getting off the bus one stop {{40}} than usual.</span>"],"r3":["SECTION II: READING SKILL (Items 31 to 60)<br>Part I: Text Completion<br>Passage 3 (Items 41 to 45)<br>Directions: Choose the best answer to complete the passage.","<span class=\"xp\">Every year, millions of tons of plastic {{41}} in the ocean. Over time, sunlight and waves break this plastic into tiny pieces called microplastics, which are often {{42}} small to see without a microscope.</span><span class=\"xp\">Scientists have found microplastics in fish, sea salt, and even drinking water, and there are growing concerns about their effect {{43}} human health. Although research is still at an early stage, many countries have already {{44}} plastic bags that are designed to be used only once.</span><span class=\"xp\">Individuals can also help by carrying their own bottles and shopping bags, {{45}} avoiding products with unnecessary packaging. Small actions, repeated by millions of people, can make a real difference.</span>"],"p1":["SECTION II: READING SKILL (Items 31 to 60)<br>Part II: Reading Comprehension<br>Passage 1 (Items 46 to 50)<br>Directions: Read the passage and choose the best answer to each question.","<span class=\"xp\">(1) Every Sunday morning, a group of teenagers in yellow shirts gathers by the Chao Phraya River in Nonthaburi. Armed with nets and gloves, they spend three hours pulling plastic bags, bottles, and foam boxes out of the water.</span><span class=\"xp\">(2) The group was started two years ago by Ton, who was only 15 at the time. One day, while fishing with his father, he caught more plastic than fish. “I was shocked,” he remembers. “I realized that if nobody did anything, the river would only get worse.”</span><span class=\"xp\">(3) At first, only three of Ton’s friends joined him. However, after he began posting short videos of their work online, more and more young people asked to take part. Today, the group has over 200 members, and several local businesses donate equipment and snacks.</span><span class=\"xp\">(4) Ton admits that cleaning the river cannot solve the problem on its own. “Most of the trash comes from people upstream,” he says. “That’s why we also visit schools to teach children not to throw waste into the river. Cleaning is important, but prevention is even more important.”</span>"],"p2":["SECTION II: READING SKILL (Items 31 to 60)<br>Part II: Reading Comprehension<br>Passage 2 (Items 51 to 55)<br>Directions: Read the passage and choose the best answer to each question.","<span class=\"xp\">(1) Octopuses have long fascinated scientists because they show surprising intelligence for animals without a backbone. They can solve puzzles, open jars to reach the food inside, and even escape from their tanks in aquariums.</span><span class=\"xp\">(2) Unlike humans, whose nerve cells are mostly in the brain, an octopus has about two thirds of its nerve cells in its eight arms. This means that each arm can, to some extent, act on its own. An arm can explore a small hole and decide whether to grab something without waiting for instructions from the brain.</span><span class=\"xp\">(3) Octopuses are also masters of disguise. Special cells in their skin allow them to change color and even texture in less than a second. This helps them hide from predators and surprise the animals they hunt.</span><span class=\"xp\">(4) Interestingly, most octopuses live for only one or two years. Some scientists believe that this short life makes their intelligence even more remarkable, since young octopuses must learn everything quickly on their own. Their mothers usually die soon after the eggs hatch, so there is no one to teach them.</span>"],"p3":["SECTION II: READING SKILL (Items 31 to 60)<br>Part II: Reading Comprehension<br>Passage 3 (Items 56 to 60)<br>Directions: Read the passage and choose the best answer to each question.","<span class=\"xp\">(1) Tourism is one of Thailand’s most important industries, bringing in billions of baht and creating millions of jobs. However, popular destinations such as the Phi Phi Islands and Chiang Mai are under growing pressure from the large number of visitors. This has led to proposals to charge foreign tourists a small fee when they enter the country.</span><span class=\"xp\">(2) Supporters say that the money could be used to protect natural sites, improve public facilities, and provide insurance for tourists who are injured or fall ill during their stay. They point out that several other countries already charge similar fees, and that a small amount is unlikely to stop people from visiting.</span><span class=\"xp\">(3) Opponents, however, worry that any extra cost could make Thailand less attractive than neighboring countries, especially for travelers on a tight budget. Some also question whether the money would really be spent on the environment, pointing to a lack of transparency in how public funds have been used in the past.</span><span class=\"xp\">(4) Both sides agree on one thing: tourism must be managed carefully if Thailand’s natural beauty is to be enjoyed by future generations. The real challenge is finding a balance between welcoming visitors and protecting the places they come to see.</span>"]},"TG70F_I":"","TG70F_P":{"qr1":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part I: Question and Response (Items 1 to 10)<br>Directions: Choose the best response to each question or statement.","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Q:</b> Would you like to join us for lunch?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>R:</b> {{1}}</span>"],"qr2":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part I: Question and Response (Items 1 to 10)<br>Directions: Choose the best response to each question or statement.","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Q:</b> Your English has improved so much!</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>R:</b> {{2}}</span>"],"qr3":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part I: Question and Response (Items 1 to 10)<br>Directions: Choose the best response to each question or statement.","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Q:</b> How long have you lived in Bangkok?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>R:</b> {{3}}</span>"],"qr4":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part I: Question and Response (Items 1 to 10)<br>Directions: Choose the best response to each question or statement.","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Q:</b> What do you do for a living?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>R:</b> {{4}}</span>"],"qr5":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part I: Question and Response (Items 1 to 10)<br>Directions: Choose the best response to each question or statement.","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Q:</b> Why don't we take a short break?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>R:</b> {{5}}</span>"],"qr6":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part I: Question and Response (Items 1 to 10)<br>Directions: Choose the best response to each question or statement.","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Q:</b> I'm going to Japan next week!</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>R:</b> {{6}}</span>"],"qr7":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part I: Question and Response (Items 1 to 10)<br>Directions: Choose the best response to each question or statement.","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Q:</b> Excuse me, does this train stop at Siam?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>R:</b> {{7}}</span>"],"qr8":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part I: Question and Response (Items 1 to 10)<br>Directions: Choose the best response to each question or statement.","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Q:</b> I can't decide what to wear to the party.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>R:</b> {{8}}</span>"],"qr9":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part I: Question and Response (Items 1 to 10)<br>Directions: Choose the best response to each question or statement.","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Q:</b> Is it all right if I pay by card?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>R:</b> {{9}}</span>"],"qr10":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part I: Question and Response (Items 1 to 10)<br>Directions: Choose the best response to each question or statement.","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Q:</b> Take care, and say hello to your family for me.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>R:</b> {{10}}</span>"],"c1":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part II: Short Conversations<br>Conversation 1 (Items 11 to 13)<br>Situation: At a bookstore","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Customer:</b> Excuse me. {{11}}?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Staff:</b> Let me check. I'm afraid it's out of stock at the moment.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Customer:</b> Oh. {{12}}?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Staff:</b> We're expecting a delivery next Tuesday.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Customer:</b> {{13}}?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Staff:</b> Of course. Can I have your name and phone number, please?</span>"],"c2":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part II: Short Conversations<br>Conversation 2 (Items 14 to 16)<br>Situation: Two students outside an exam room","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Nina:</b> You look worried. {{14}}?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Tee:</b> Not really. I didn't sleep well last night, and the exam starts in ten minutes.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Nina:</b> {{15}}.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Tee:</b> You're right. I know the material well. I just need to relax.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Nina:</b> {{16}}!</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Tee:</b> Thanks. You too!</span>"],"c3":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part II: Short Conversations<br>Conversation 3 (Items 17 to 20)<br>Situation: At a bicycle rental shop","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Customer:</b> Hi. {{17}}.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Staff:</b> Sure. It's 150 baht for the whole day, or 40 baht an hour.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Customer:</b> {{18}}.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Staff:</b> Great. I'll need a deposit of 500 baht, which you'll get back when you return the bike.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Customer:</b> {{19}}?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Staff:</b> Yes, it is. We also give you a lock and a map of the old town.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Customer:</b> {{20}}?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Staff:</b> We close at 7 p.m., so please return it before then.</span>"],"c4":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part III: Long Conversations<br>Conversation 1 (Items 21 to 25)<br>Situation: A student asks a librarian for help with a project","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Librarian:</b> Hi there. {{21}}?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Student:</b> Yes, please. I'm doing a project on climate change in Thailand, but I'm not sure where to start.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Librarian:</b> {{22}}.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Student:</b> Do I need a password to use it?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Librarian:</b> {{23}}.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Student:</b> Great. What if I need printed books as well?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Librarian:</b> {{24}}.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Student:</b> Can I borrow them for a week?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Librarian:</b> {{25}}.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Student:</b> Perfect. Thank you so much for your help.</span>"],"c5":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part III: Long Conversations<br>Conversation 2 (Items 26 to 30)<br>Situation: Two friends talking about adopting a pet","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Pim:</b> I'm thinking of adopting a dog from the animal shelter. {{26}}?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Ben:</b> That's a great idea, but have you thought about who will take care of it while you're at school?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Pim:</b> {{27}}.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Ben:</b> That's good. Dogs need a lot of attention.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Pim:</b> I know. {{28}}.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Ben:</b> That's a big commitment. Are you sure you can get up that early every day?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Pim:</b> {{29}}.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Ben:</b> Well, it sounds like you've really thought it through.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Pim:</b> {{30}}?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Ben:</b> Sure. I'd love to help you choose one.</span>"],"r1":["SECTION II: READING SKILL (Items 31 to 60)<br>Part I: Text Completion<br>Passage 1 (Items 31 to 35)<br>Directions: Choose the best answer to complete the passage.","<span class=\"xp\">Muay Thai, the national martial art of Thailand, {{31}} as “the art of eight limbs” because fighters use their fists, elbows, knees, and shins. It has a long history {{32}} hundreds of years, when it was used by soldiers on the battlefield.</span><span class=\"xp\">Today, there are Muay Thai gyms in many countries around the world, and {{33}} foreign visitors come to Thailand every year to train with local teachers. Many of them say that the sport teaches not only fitness but also respect and discipline.</span><span class=\"xp\">Before each fight, boxers perform a traditional dance called the <b>wai khru</b> to {{34}} their teachers and ask for protection. For many Thais, the worldwide popularity of Muay Thai has become a great source of national {{35}}.</span>"],"r2":["SECTION II: READING SKILL (Items 31 to 60)<br>Part I: Text Completion<br>Passage 2 (Items 36 to 40)<br>Directions: Choose the best answer to complete the passage.","<span class=\"xp\">Online courses have made education more {{36}} than ever before. Students can now learn from top universities {{37}} where they live, and many courses are free or very cheap.</span><span class=\"xp\">However, research has found that only a small percentage of learners who start these courses actually {{38}} them. One reason is that it is easy to lose motivation when nobody is {{39}} your progress.</span><span class=\"xp\">Experts therefore recommend setting a regular study schedule and joining online study groups {{40}} learners feel more connected and responsible to others.</span>"],"r3":["SECTION II: READING SKILL (Items 31 to 60)<br>Part I: Text Completion<br>Passage 3 (Items 41 to 45)<br>Directions: Choose the best answer to complete the passage.","<span class=\"xp\">Thailand is {{41}} becoming an aged society. In the coming years, a much larger share of the population will be over the age of sixty. This change is mainly {{42}} falling birth rates and longer life expectancy.</span><span class=\"xp\">As the number of people of working age {{43}}, the country may face a shortage of workers. At the same time, the government will be under pressure to provide health care and income support for {{44}}.</span><span class=\"xp\">Some experts suggest encouraging older people to keep working for longer, {{45}} others focus on attracting more young families by offering better childcare and parental leave.</span>"],"p1":["SECTION II: READING SKILL (Items 31 to 60)<br>Part II: Reading Comprehension<br>Passage 1 (Items 46 to 50)<br>Directions: Read the passage and choose the best answer to each question.","<span class=\"xp\">(1) When Fon took over her family’s rice farm in Yasothon, she inherited debts as well as land. Like many farmers in the area, her parents had relied on chemical fertilizers that became more expensive every year, while the price of rice stayed low.</span><span class=\"xp\">(2) Fon decided to try organic farming, a choice that her neighbors considered risky. In the first year, her harvest fell by almost a third, and some villagers told her that she would soon give up. Instead, she used her smartphone to learn from organic farmers online and to keep careful records of the soil on her fields.</span><span class=\"xp\">(3) Slowly, the soil grew healthier, and her harvests recovered. More importantly, Fon began selling her rice directly to customers in Bangkok through social media, at a price nearly twice what the local rice mill had paid. Within five years, she had paid off all of her family’s debts.</span><span class=\"xp\">(4) Fon now runs free workshops for farmers in her district. “I’m not telling them to copy me,” she says. “I just want them to know that they have more choices than they think.”</span>"],"p2":["SECTION II: READING SKILL (Items 31 to 60)<br>Part II: Reading Comprehension<br>Passage 2 (Items 51 to 55)<br>Directions: Read the passage and choose the best answer to each question.","<span class=\"xp\">(1) Coral reefs cover less than one percent of the ocean floor, yet they support about a quarter of all marine species. Although corals may look like plants, they are actually animals. They get much of their food from tiny algae that live inside their bodies, and these algae also give corals their bright colors.</span><span class=\"xp\">(2) When the ocean becomes too warm, corals become stressed and push the algae out. Without the algae, the corals turn white, a process known as bleaching. Bleached corals are not dead, but they are weak. If the water stays warm for too long, they may starve and eventually die.</span><span class=\"xp\">(3) In recent years, rising sea temperatures have caused large bleaching events around the world, including in the Andaman Sea and the Gulf of Thailand. In some areas, Thai authorities have temporarily closed popular diving spots to give the reefs time to recover.</span><span class=\"xp\">(4) Scientists stress that reducing carbon emissions around the world is the only lasting solution. In the meantime, local actions, such as limiting the number of tourists and avoiding sunscreen that contains harmful chemicals, can help reefs survive.</span>"],"p3":["SECTION II: READING SKILL (Items 31 to 60)<br>Part II: Reading Comprehension<br>Passage 3 (Items 56 to 60)<br>Directions: Read the passage and choose the best answer to each question.","<span class=\"xp\">(1) In recent years, many countries have added coding, or computer programming, to their primary school curriculum. Thailand, too, has made computing science part of its national curriculum. Supporters see this as essential preparation for a digital future.</span><span class=\"xp\">(2) Supporters argue that coding teaches more than how to write programs. It trains children to break big problems into smaller steps, to think logically, and to keep trying when something does not work. These skills, they say, are useful in any career, not just in technology.</span><span class=\"xp\">(3) Critics agree that these skills are valuable, but they question whether young children should spend limited school time in front of screens. They argue that reading, mathematics, and play are still the foundation of learning, and that coding can wait until secondary school. Others point out that many schools lack trained teachers and computers, which may widen the gap between rich and poor schools.</span><span class=\"xp\">(4) Perhaps the answer lies in how coding is taught. Some teachers now use “unplugged” activities, such as games and puzzles that require no computers, to teach the same ways of thinking. This approach may offer the benefits of coding without the drawbacks of extra screen time.</span>"]},"TG70G_I":"","TG70G_P":{"qr1":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part I: Question and Response (Items 1 to 10)<br>Directions: Choose the best response to each question or statement.","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Q:</b> Could you pass me the salt, please?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>R:</b> {{1}}</span>"],"qr2":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part I: Question and Response (Items 1 to 10)<br>Directions: Choose the best response to each question or statement.","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Q:</b> Excuse me, I think you're sitting in my seat.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>R:</b> {{2}}</span>"],"qr3":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part I: Question and Response (Items 1 to 10)<br>Directions: Choose the best response to each question or statement.","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Q:</b> How far is the hospital from here?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>R:</b> {{3}}</span>"],"qr4":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part I: Question and Response (Items 1 to 10)<br>Directions: Choose the best response to each question or statement.","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Q:</b> Have you heard the news about Mint?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>R:</b> {{4}}</span>"],"qr5":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part I: Question and Response (Items 1 to 10)<br>Directions: Choose the best response to each question or statement.","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Q:</b> You look exhausted. Are you all right?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>R:</b> {{5}}</span>"],"qr6":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part I: Question and Response (Items 1 to 10)<br>Directions: Choose the best response to each question or statement.","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Q:</b> What a beautiful view!</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>R:</b> {{6}}</span>"],"qr7":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part I: Question and Response (Items 1 to 10)<br>Directions: Choose the best response to each question or statement.","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Q:</b> Would it be possible to move my appointment to Friday?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>R:</b> {{7}}</span>"],"qr8":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part I: Question and Response (Items 1 to 10)<br>Directions: Choose the best response to each question or statement.","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Q:</b> Oh no, I've lost my keys again!</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>R:</b> {{8}}</span>"],"qr9":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part I: Question and Response (Items 1 to 10)<br>Directions: Choose the best response to each question or statement.","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Q:</b> Who does this umbrella belong to?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>R:</b> {{9}}</span>"],"qr10":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part I: Question and Response (Items 1 to 10)<br>Directions: Choose the best response to each question or statement.","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Q:</b> Thank you for having me. I had a wonderful time.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>R:</b> {{10}}</span>"],"c1":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part II: Short Conversations<br>Conversation 1 (Items 11 to 13)<br>Situation: At a mobile phone repair shop","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Customer:</b> Hi. {{11}}.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Staff:</b> Let me take a look. Hmm, it'll need a new screen.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Customer:</b> {{12}}?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Staff:</b> About two hours. You can pick it up after lunch.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Customer:</b> {{13}}?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Staff:</b> Yes, don't worry. We won't touch any of your data.</span>"],"c2":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part II: Short Conversations<br>Conversation 2 (Items 14 to 16)<br>Situation: Two friends deciding where to have lunch","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Ploy:</b> I'm starving. {{14}}?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Gun:</b> How about that new Korean restaurant near the station?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Ploy:</b> {{15}}.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Gun:</b> Then let's go now, before the lunch rush starts.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Ploy:</b> {{16}}.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Gun:</b> Great. I'll call a taxi.</span>"],"c3":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part II: Short Conversations<br>Conversation 3 (Items 17 to 20)<br>Situation: At an airport information desk","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Traveler:</b> Excuse me. {{17}}?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Staff:</b> Go out through Gate 4 and turn right. The bus stop is just outside.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Traveler:</b> {{18}}?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Staff:</b> Every twenty minutes until midnight.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Traveler:</b> {{19}}?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Staff:</b> You can pay the driver, but it's cheaper to buy a ticket from the machine over there.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Traveler:</b> {{20}}</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Staff:</b> You're welcome. Enjoy your stay.</span>"],"c4":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part III: Long Conversations<br>Conversation 1 (Items 21 to 25)<br>Situation: A student talks to his teacher after class","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Teacher:</b> Hi, Krit. {{21}}?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Krit:</b> Well, I'm afraid I won't be able to hand in my essay on time.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Teacher:</b> I see. {{22}}?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Krit:</b> My grandmother was in hospital, and I spent the whole weekend helping my family.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Teacher:</b> {{23}}?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Krit:</b> Yes, she's back home now. Thank you for asking.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Teacher:</b> {{24}}.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Krit:</b> Thank you so much. I'll hand it in by Thursday.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Teacher:</b> {{25}}.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Krit:</b> I will. Thanks again.</span>"],"c5":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part III: Long Conversations<br>Conversation 2 (Items 26 to 30)<br>Situation: Two coworkers talking about a charity event","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Dan:</b> Our company is organizing a charity run next month. {{26}}?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>May:</b> I'd like to, but I haven't run in years.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Dan:</b> {{27}}.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>May:</b> Five kilometers? I think I can manage that.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Dan:</b> {{28}}.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>May:</b> Good idea. How about Tuesdays and Thursdays?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Dan:</b> {{29}}.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>May:</b> Perfect. By the way, where does the money go?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Dan:</b> {{30}}.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>May:</b> That's wonderful. Count me in!</span>"],"r1":["SECTION II: READING SKILL (Items 31 to 60)<br>Part I: Text Completion<br>Passage 1 (Items 31 to 35)<br>Directions: Choose the best answer to complete the passage.","<span class=\"xp\">Loy Krathong is one of the most beautiful festivals in Thailand. On the night of the full moon in the twelfth lunar month, people {{31}} small decorated baskets on rivers and canals to give thanks to the water goddess and to let go of bad luck.</span><span class=\"xp\">Traditionally, the baskets, called <b>krathong</b>, {{32}} from banana leaves and the trunks of banana trees. In recent decades, however, many people began to use foam krathong, which take a very long time to break down and {{33}} rivers and canals.</span><span class=\"xp\">To reduce the problem, some cities now encourage people to use krathong made of bread, which can be {{34}} by fish. Others suggest that families share one krathong {{35}} each person floating their own.</span>"],"r2":["SECTION II: READING SKILL (Items 31 to 60)<br>Part I: Text Completion<br>Passage 2 (Items 36 to 40)<br>Directions: Choose the best answer to complete the passage.","<span class=\"xp\">Electric vehicles, or EVs, are becoming a common sight on Thai roads. Sales have risen {{36}} in recent years, partly {{37}} government support and falling prices.</span><span class=\"xp\">Many EV owners say that their cars are cheaper to run {{38}} petrol cars, and that they enjoy the quiet, smooth ride. However, some drivers still hesitate to buy one because there are not {{39}} charging stations outside big cities, especially along long highways.</span><span class=\"xp\">Experts believe that if charging networks continue to expand, EVs {{40}} a major role in reducing air pollution in Thai cities over the next decade.</span>"],"r3":["SECTION II: READING SKILL (Items 31 to 60)<br>Part I: Text Completion<br>Passage 3 (Items 41 to 45)<br>Directions: Choose the best answer to complete the passage.","<span class=\"xp\">{{41}} mistakes is a natural part of learning, yet many people are terrified of failure. Some of the most successful inventors in history failed hundreds of times {{42}} they finally created something that worked.</span><span class=\"xp\">Psychologists say that people who see mistakes as chances to learn are more likely to {{43}} trying when things get difficult. In contrast, people who fear failure often avoid challenges completely, {{44}} limits their personal growth.</span><span class=\"xp\">So the next time you make a mistake, instead of feeling ashamed, try asking yourself {{45}} you can learn from it.</span>"],"p1":["SECTION II: READING SKILL (Items 31 to 60)<br>Part II: Reading Comprehension<br>Passage 1 (Items 46 to 50)<br>Directions: Read the passage and choose the best answer to each question.","<span class=\"xp\">(1) Every morning at seven, Nurse Jin steps onto a small boat at a pier in Trat. Her destination is one of several small islands where there are no hospitals and only a few hundred residents, many of whom are elderly.</span><span class=\"xp\">(2) Jin has worked as a community nurse for eleven years. On each island, she checks blood pressure, gives vaccinations, and teaches families how to care for patients who cannot leave their beds. “Without these visits, many of my patients would have to spend a whole day traveling to the mainland just to see a doctor,” she explains.</span><span class=\"xp\">(3) The job is not easy. During the rainy season, strong waves sometimes make the trip too dangerous, and Jin has to cancel her visits. To stay in touch, she has trained a volunteer on each island to use a tablet for video calls, so that patients can still talk to her when the weather is bad.</span><span class=\"xp\">(4) Jin says that the hardest part of her job is not the sea, but saying goodbye. “When you visit the same people for years, they become more than patients,” she says. “They become part of your life.”</span>"],"p2":["SECTION II: READING SKILL (Items 31 to 60)<br>Part II: Reading Comprehension<br>Passage 2 (Items 51 to 55)<br>Directions: Read the passage and choose the best answer to each question.","<span class=\"xp\">(1) Elephants are famous for their loud trumpeting calls, but much of their communication actually happens at sounds too low for humans to hear. These deep rumbles, known as infrasound, can travel several kilometers through the air and even through the ground.</span><span class=\"xp\">(2) Scientists believe that elephants detect these vibrations not only with their ears but also through their feet. Special cells in the soles of their feet are very sensitive to movement in the ground, allowing elephants to “hear” messages from far away.</span><span class=\"xp\">(3) Researchers have found that elephants use these rumbles to keep their herds together, to warn one another of danger, and to find partners. Mothers and their young also use them to stay in contact when they become separated.</span><span class=\"xp\">(4) Understanding how elephants communicate may help to protect them. In some areas, sensors that pick up elephant rumbles have been tested as a way to warn villagers when a herd is approaching their farms. Such systems could reduce dangerous conflicts between people and elephants.</span>"],"p3":["SECTION II: READING SKILL (Items 31 to 60)<br>Part II: Reading Comprehension<br>Passage 3 (Items 56 to 60)<br>Directions: Read the passage and choose the best answer to each question.","<span class=\"xp\">(1) In many buffet restaurants in Thailand, where customers pay one price and eat as much as they like, signs warn that customers will be charged extra for leaving food on their plates. The rule has become a topic of lively debate among diners.</span><span class=\"xp\">(2) Supporters say the charge is a fair way to reduce food waste. Buffet customers often take more than they can eat, and the leftover food is usually thrown away. A small charge, they argue, encourages people to think carefully before filling their plates.</span><span class=\"xp\">(3) Critics, on the other hand, say the rule can be applied unfairly. Some customers may leave food because it is too spicy or not cooked properly, not because they were greedy. Others feel that once they have paid, they should not be told how much to eat.</span><span class=\"xp\">(4) Some restaurants have tried a different approach. Instead of punishing waste, they reward customers who finish everything with a small discount or a free dessert. Early results suggest that this positive method reduces waste just as well, while keeping customers much happier.</span>"]},"TG70H_I":"","TG70H_P":{"qr1":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part I: Question and Response (Items 1 to 10)<br>Directions: Choose the best response to each question or statement.","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Q:</b> Do you want to share a taxi to the airport?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>R:</b> {{1}}</span>"],"qr2":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part I: Question and Response (Items 1 to 10)<br>Directions: Choose the best response to each question or statement.","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Q:</b> Excuse me, is there an ATM anywhere near here?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>R:</b> {{2}}</span>"],"qr3":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part I: Question and Response (Items 1 to 10)<br>Directions: Choose the best response to each question or statement.","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Q:</b> How much do I owe you for the concert ticket?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>R:</b> {{3}}</span>"],"qr4":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part I: Question and Response (Items 1 to 10)<br>Directions: Choose the best response to each question or statement.","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Q:</b> Would you prefer tea or coffee?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>R:</b> {{4}}</span>"],"qr5":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part I: Question and Response (Items 1 to 10)<br>Directions: Choose the best response to each question or statement.","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Q:</b> I'm thinking about getting a cat.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>R:</b> {{5}}</span>"],"qr6":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part I: Question and Response (Items 1 to 10)<br>Directions: Choose the best response to each question or statement.","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Q:</b> Can I get a refund for this shirt? It doesn't fit.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>R:</b> {{6}}</span>"],"qr7":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part I: Question and Response (Items 1 to 10)<br>Directions: Choose the best response to each question or statement.","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Q:</b> When is the history assignment due?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>R:</b> {{7}}</span>"],"qr8":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part I: Question and Response (Items 1 to 10)<br>Directions: Choose the best response to each question or statement.","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Q:</b> I'm sorry, I didn't catch your name.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>R:</b> {{8}}</span>"],"qr9":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part I: Question and Response (Items 1 to 10)<br>Directions: Choose the best response to each question or statement.","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Q:</b> Shall we meet at the main gate at six?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>R:</b> {{9}}</span>"],"qr10":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part I: Question and Response (Items 1 to 10)<br>Directions: Choose the best response to each question or statement.","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Q:</b> How did you like the movie?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>R:</b> {{10}}</span>"],"c1":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part II: Short Conversations<br>Conversation 1 (Items 11 to 13)<br>Situation: A phone call to an animal clinic","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Receptionist:</b> Good morning, Happy Paws Animal Clinic.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Customer:</b> Hi. {{11}}.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Receptionist:</b> Oh, I'm sorry to hear that. Can you bring her in at four this afternoon?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Customer:</b> {{12}}?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Receptionist:</b> Let me see. We have an opening at 11:30 this morning.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Customer:</b> {{13}}.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Receptionist:</b> Great. Please bring her in a pet carrier.</span>"],"c2":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part II: Short Conversations<br>Conversation 2 (Items 14 to 16)<br>Situation: Two friends talking about a book","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Fern:</b> {{14}}?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Oat:</b> Almost. I have just two chapters left.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Fern:</b> {{15}}.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Oat:</b> Okay. I'll give it back to you on Friday.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Fern:</b> {{16}}?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Oat:</b> I love it. I can’t wait to see how it ends.</span>"],"c3":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part II: Short Conversations<br>Conversation 3 (Items 17 to 20)<br>Situation: At a hair salon","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Stylist:</b> Hi there. What would you like today?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Customer:</b> {{17}}.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Stylist:</b> Sure. How much would you like me to cut off?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Customer:</b> {{18}}.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Stylist:</b> Got it. Would you like a hair wash as well?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Customer:</b> {{19}}?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Stylist:</b> It's 100 baht more.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Customer:</b> {{20}}.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Stylist:</b> Great. Please follow me to the sink.</span>"],"c4":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part III: Long Conversations<br>Conversation 1 (Items 21 to 25)<br>Situation: A student calls a university scholarship office","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Officer:</b> Good afternoon, International Scholarship Office.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Student:</b> Hi. {{21}}.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Officer:</b> Sure. Are you a current university student?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Student:</b> {{22}}.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Officer:</b> Great. Then you are eligible to apply. Have you taken a Japanese language test?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Student:</b> {{23}}.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Officer:</b> That's fine. You can submit the result later.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Student:</b> {{24}}?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Officer:</b> The end of January. All documents must be uploaded online.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Student:</b> {{25}}.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Officer:</b> Good luck with your application!</span>"],"c5":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part III: Long Conversations<br>Conversation 2 (Items 26 to 30)<br>Situation: Two friends talking about buying a used motorbike","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Nat:</b> {{26}}?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Joe:</b> Not yet. The price is good, but I'm a little worried.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Nat:</b> {{27}}?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Joe:</b> The seller won't let me take it to a mechanic first.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Nat:</b> {{28}}.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Joe:</b> That's exactly what I was thinking.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Nat:</b> {{29}}?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Joe:</b> Good idea. Do you know any good shops?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Nat:</b> {{30}}.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Joe:</b> Great. Can you send me the address?</span>"],"r1":["SECTION II: READING SKILL (Items 31 to 60)<br>Part I: Text Completion<br>Passage 1 (Items 31 to 35)<br>Directions: Choose the best answer to complete the passage.","<span class=\"xp\">Songkran, the Thai New Year, falls {{31}} the middle of April, the hottest time of the year. Today, it is best known for its huge water fights, {{32}} people of all ages splash water on friends and strangers alike.</span><span class=\"xp\">Originally, however, Songkran was a quiet time for families. People {{33}} pour scented water gently over the hands of older relatives as a sign of respect and to ask for their blessings.</span><span class=\"xp\">{{34}} the festival has become more commercial in recent years, many Thais still travel back to their hometowns during Songkran to {{35}} respect to their elders and spend time with their families.</span>"],"r2":["SECTION II: READING SKILL (Items 31 to 60)<br>Part I: Text Completion<br>Passage 2 (Items 36 to 40)<br>Directions: Choose the best answer to complete the passage.","<span class=\"xp\">Studies show that children who read for pleasure {{36}} to do better at school, not only in language subjects {{37}} in mathematics. This may be because regular reading improves concentration and {{38}} vocabulary.</span><span class=\"xp\">However, the number of young people who read books for fun has {{39}} in recent years, as more of their free time is spent on phones and games.</span><span class=\"xp\">To reverse this trend, experts suggest that parents {{40}} with their children every day, even for just fifteen minutes, and let them choose books that match their own interests.</span>"],"r3":["SECTION II: READING SKILL (Items 31 to 60)<br>Part I: Text Completion<br>Passage 3 (Items 41 to 45)<br>Directions: Choose the best answer to complete the passage.","<span class=\"xp\">In recent years, many companies have allowed their employees to work from home for {{41}} part of the week. Many workers say they are more productive at home because they {{42}} have to spend hours commuting in heavy traffic.</span><span class=\"xp\">However, others find it difficult to separate work from home life, and some feel {{43}} after spending long periods without seeing their colleagues. Managers also worry that teamwork may suffer when people rarely meet {{44}}.</span><span class=\"xp\">As a result, many companies now use a hybrid model, {{45}} employees spend some days in the office and the rest at home.</span>"],"p1":["SECTION II: READING SKILL (Items 31 to 60)<br>Part II: Reading Comprehension<br>Passage 1 (Items 46 to 50)<br>Directions: Read the passage and choose the best answer to each question.","<span class=\"xp\">(1) When Ohm lost the use of his legs in a car accident at the age of nineteen, he believed his days of traveling were over. For almost a year, he rarely left his apartment in Khon Kaen.</span><span class=\"xp\">(2) Everything changed when a close friend persuaded him to join a trip to Chiang Mai. The journey was full of challenges. Some hotels had no ramps, and many tourist attractions could only be reached by stairs. Yet Ohm discovered that he still loved exploring new places.</span><span class=\"xp\">(3) After the trip, he started an online channel that reviews travel destinations from the point of view of a wheelchair user. He rates hotels, restaurants, and attractions on how easy they are to access, and he shares honest videos of both good and bad experiences.</span><span class=\"xp\">(4) Today, his channel has more than 100,000 followers. Several hotels have even added ramps and accessible bathrooms after appearing in his videos. “I’m not trying to make people feel sorry for me,” Ohm says. “I just want everyone to be able to travel.”</span>"],"p2":["SECTION II: READING SKILL (Items 31 to 60)<br>Part II: Reading Comprehension<br>Passage 2 (Items 51 to 55)<br>Directions: Read the passage and choose the best answer to each question.","<span class=\"xp\">(1) Chili peppers are an essential part of Thai cooking, but why do they make our mouths feel as if they are on fire? The answer lies in a chemical called capsaicin, which is found mainly in the white parts inside the pepper.</span><span class=\"xp\">(2) Capsaicin attaches to sensors in our mouths that normally detect heat. When these sensors are activated, they send a message to the brain that the mouth is burning, even though its temperature has not actually changed. In other words, the “heat” of chili is an illusion created by the brain.</span><span class=\"xp\">(3) Interestingly, drinking water does little to cool the burning feeling, because capsaicin does not mix well with water. Milk and yogurt are much more effective, since they contain a protein that helps wash capsaicin away.</span><span class=\"xp\">(4) Scientists believe that chili plants produce capsaicin to protect their seeds from mammals, which tend to destroy seeds when they chew. Birds, however, cannot feel the burn, so they happily eat the peppers and spread the seeds far and wide.</span>"],"p3":["SECTION II: READING SKILL (Items 31 to 60)<br>Part II: Reading Comprehension<br>Passage 3 (Items 56 to 60)<br>Directions: Read the passage and choose the best answer to each question.","<span class=\"xp\">(1) Some universities require students to complete a certain number of hours of community service before they can graduate. Supporters see this as a valuable part of education, while critics question whether service should ever be forced on anyone.</span><span class=\"xp\">(2) Supporters argue that community service helps students apply what they learn in class to real problems. It also develops empathy and teamwork, which are difficult to teach in a lecture hall. In addition, many employers value graduates who have experience working with people from different backgrounds.</span><span class=\"xp\">(3) Critics, however, argue that requiring service turns a generous act into an obligation. Students who are forced to volunteer may simply complete the hours without caring about the people they are supposed to help. Moreover, students who work part time to pay for their studies may find it hard to fit extra hours into their busy schedules.</span><span class=\"xp\">(4) A possible solution is to give students more choice. Instead of setting a fixed number of hours, universities could allow students to choose projects linked to their own interests and fields of study. This way, service could become a meaningful experience rather than just another requirement.</span>"]},"TG70I_I":"","TG70I_P":{"qr1":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part I: Question and Response (Items 1 to 10)<br>Directions: Choose the best response to each question or statement.","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Q:</b> Can you give me a ride to the station?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>R:</b> {{1}}</span>"],"qr2":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part I: Question and Response (Items 1 to 10)<br>Directions: Choose the best response to each question or statement.","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Q:</b> Do you have any plans for the long weekend?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>R:</b> {{2}}</span>"],"qr3":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part I: Question and Response (Items 1 to 10)<br>Directions: Choose the best response to each question or statement.","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Q:</b> What time does the bank open?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>R:</b> {{3}}</span>"],"qr4":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part I: Question and Response (Items 1 to 10)<br>Directions: Choose the best response to each question or statement.","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Q:</b> Is it okay to take photos in here?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>R:</b> {{4}}</span>"],"qr5":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part I: Question and Response (Items 1 to 10)<br>Directions: Choose the best response to each question or statement.","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Q:</b> You've got a good point there.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>R:</b> {{5}}</span>"],"qr6":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part I: Question and Response (Items 1 to 10)<br>Directions: Choose the best response to each question or statement.","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Q:</b> Guess what? I passed my driving test!</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>R:</b> {{6}}</span>"],"qr7":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part I: Question and Response (Items 1 to 10)<br>Directions: Choose the best response to each question or statement.","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Q:</b> Could you speak a little more slowly, please?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>R:</b> {{7}}</span>"],"qr8":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part I: Question and Response (Items 1 to 10)<br>Directions: Choose the best response to each question or statement.","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Q:</b> Where did you get that nice bag?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>R:</b> {{8}}</span>"],"qr9":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part I: Question and Response (Items 1 to 10)<br>Directions: Choose the best response to each question or statement.","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Q:</b> What do you usually do in your free time?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>R:</b> {{9}}</span>"],"qr10":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part I: Question and Response (Items 1 to 10)<br>Directions: Choose the best response to each question or statement.","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Q:</b> I'd better be going. It's getting late.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>R:</b> {{10}}</span>"],"c1":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part II: Short Conversations<br>Conversation 1 (Items 11 to 13)<br>Situation: At a clothing store","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Customer:</b> Excuse me. {{11}}?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Staff:</b> Let me check. Yes, we have it in large and extra large.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Customer:</b> {{12}}?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Staff:</b> Sure. The fitting room is at the back of the store.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Customer:</b> It fits perfectly. {{13}}?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Staff:</b> Yes, of course. Just scan this code here.</span>"],"c2":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part II: Short Conversations<br>Conversation 2 (Items 14 to 16)<br>Situation: Two neighbors in an apartment building","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Mali:</b> Hi, Jo. I'm going away for a week. {{14}}?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Jo:</b> Sure, no problem. How often do they need water?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Mali:</b> {{15}}.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Jo:</b> Okay. Where should I get the key?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Mali:</b> {{16}}.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Jo:</b> Got it. Have a nice trip!</span>"],"c3":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part II: Short Conversations<br>Conversation 3 (Items 17 to 20)<br>Situation: At a museum ticket counter","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Visitor:</b> Hi. {{17}}?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Staff:</b> It's 200 baht for adults, but students get in for half price.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Visitor:</b> {{18}}.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Staff:</b> Great, that's 100 baht, then.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Visitor:</b> {{19}}?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Staff:</b> Yes, there's one at two o'clock, and it's free.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Visitor:</b> {{20}}.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Staff:</b> Wonderful. Please meet the guide in the main hall.</span>"],"c4":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part III: Long Conversations<br>Conversation 1 (Items 21 to 25)<br>Situation: A customer calls an internet service provider","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Agent:</b> Thank you for calling FastNet. This is Ploy.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Customer:</b> Hi. {{21}}.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Agent:</b> I'm sorry to hear that. Have you tried restarting your router?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Customer:</b> {{22}}.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Agent:</b> I see. Let me check your line. Could I have your account number, please?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Customer:</b> {{23}}.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Agent:</b> Thank you. It looks like there is a problem with the cable in your area.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Customer:</b> {{24}}?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Agent:</b> Our technicians should finish the repair by tomorrow evening.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Customer:</b> {{25}}?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Agent:</b> Yes. We'll take two days off your next bill.</span>"],"c5":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part III: Long Conversations<br>Conversation 2 (Items 26 to 30)<br>Situation: Two university students talking about clubs","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Ice:</b> {{26}}?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Tan:</b> Not yet. I'm thinking about the photography club or the volunteer club.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Ice:</b> {{27}}?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Tan:</b> I love taking pictures, but I also want to do something useful for society.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Ice:</b> {{28}}?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Tan:</b> That's a great idea! Then I could do both.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Ice:</b> {{29}}.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Tan:</b> Really? What kind of activities do you do?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Ice:</b> {{30}}.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Tan:</b> That sounds wonderful. Sign me up!</span>"],"r1":["SECTION II: READING SKILL (Items 31 to 60)<br>Part I: Text Completion<br>Passage 1 (Items 31 to 35)<br>Directions: Choose the best answer to complete the passage.","<span class=\"xp\">The BTS Skytrain, which {{31}} in 1999, was the first mass transit rail system in Bangkok. Before it was built, many commuters spent hours {{32}} in traffic every day.</span><span class=\"xp\">Since then, the rail network has expanded to {{33}} much of the city, with several new lines connecting the suburbs to the city center.</span><span class=\"xp\">Although fares are {{34}} high for some low income workers, the trains remain popular {{35}} their speed and reliability, especially during the rush hour.</span>"],"r2":["SECTION II: READING SKILL (Items 31 to 60)<br>Part I: Text Completion<br>Passage 2 (Items 36 to 40)<br>Directions: Choose the best answer to complete the passage.","<span class=\"xp\">Chinese has become one of the most popular foreign languages {{36}} Thai students. This trend {{37}} the growing trade and tourism links between the two countries.</span><span class=\"xp\">Many students believe that being able to speak Chinese {{38}} them an advantage in the job market. However, learning the language is not easy, as thousands of written characters must be memorized.</span><span class=\"xp\">On the other hand, Chinese is a {{39}} language, like Thai, which may make pronunciation {{40}} for Thai learners than for native speakers of English.</span>"],"r3":["SECTION II: READING SKILL (Items 31 to 60)<br>Part I: Text Completion<br>Passage 3 (Items 41 to 45)<br>Directions: Choose the best answer to complete the passage.","<span class=\"xp\">Space tourism, once only a dream, is slowly becoming {{41}}. Several private companies have already sent paying customers {{42}} space for short trips.</span><span class=\"xp\">However, tickets are extremely expensive, often costing {{43}} of dollars. Critics argue that this money {{44}} on solving problems on Earth, such as poverty and climate change.</span><span class=\"xp\">Supporters, on the other hand, believe that space tourism could lead to new technologies {{45}} benefit everyone, just as earlier space programs led to inventions that we now use every day.</span>"],"p1":["SECTION II: READING SKILL (Items 31 to 60)<br>Part II: Reading Comprehension<br>Passage 1 (Items 46 to 50)<br>Directions: Read the passage and choose the best answer to each question.","<span class=\"xp\">(1) For most of her life, Somsri could not read. She grew up in a poor farming family in Buriram and left school at the age of eight to help her parents in the rice fields. As an adult, she kept her secret by asking others to read signs and documents for her.</span><span class=\"xp\">(2) At the age of 70, Somsri decided it was time for a change. She joined a free adult reading class at the local temple, where a retired teacher taught a small group of older learners twice a week.</span><span class=\"xp\">(3) Progress was slow at first. Her eyes tired quickly, and she often forgot letters she had learned the day before. But she practiced every evening by reading the labels on food packages and the headlines in the newspaper.</span><span class=\"xp\">(4) Two years later, Somsri can read short stories to her grandchildren. “I used to feel ashamed,” she says. “Now I feel as if a new door has opened in front of me.”</span>"],"p2":["SECTION II: READING SKILL (Items 31 to 60)<br>Part II: Reading Comprehension<br>Passage 2 (Items 51 to 55)<br>Directions: Read the passage and choose the best answer to each question.","<span class=\"xp\">(1) Everyone yawns, from newborn babies to the elderly, and many animals do it too. Yet scientists still do not fully understand why we yawn.</span><span class=\"xp\">(2) One popular explanation is that yawning increases the amount of oxygen in the blood. However, experiments have shown that breathing extra oxygen does not make people yawn less, so this explanation is now considered unlikely.</span><span class=\"xp\">(3) A newer idea suggests that yawning helps to cool the brain. When we yawn, we take in a large amount of air, which may lower the temperature of the blood flowing to the brain. Supporting this idea, studies have found that people yawn more often in mild weather than in very hot or very cold conditions.</span><span class=\"xp\">(4) Yawning is also contagious. Simply seeing, hearing, or even reading about someone yawning can make us yawn too. Interestingly, people are more likely to “catch” yawns from friends and family than from strangers, which suggests that contagious yawning may be linked to empathy.</span>"],"p3":["SECTION II: READING SKILL (Items 31 to 60)<br>Part II: Reading Comprehension<br>Passage 3 (Items 56 to 60)<br>Directions: Read the passage and choose the best answer to each question.","<span class=\"xp\">(1) Childhood obesity has become a growing concern in Thailand. In response, some schools have banned the sale of sweet drinks, such as soda and sweetened milk tea, on campus. The policy has divided parents and teachers.</span><span class=\"xp\">(2) Supporters say the ban is a simple and effective way to reduce how much sugar children consume. Habits formed in childhood often last a lifetime, so schools should be places that encourage healthy choices. They also point out that Thailand already taxes sugary drinks, showing that the government takes the issue seriously.</span><span class=\"xp\">(3) Opponents, however, argue that a ban achieves little if children can simply buy the same drinks from shops right outside the school gate. They believe that education is more effective than rules. Teaching children to read nutrition labels and understand the effects of sugar, they say, will help them make better choices on their own.</span><span class=\"xp\">(4) Many experts suggest combining both approaches. “Rules can change what children drink at school,” one nutritionist explains, “but only knowledge can change what they choose for the rest of their lives.”</span>"]},"TG70J_I":"","TG70J_P":{"qr1":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part I: Question and Response (Items 1 to 10)<br>Directions: Choose the best response to each question or statement.","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Q:</b> Excuse me, which way is the restroom?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>R:</b> {{1}}</span>"],"qr2":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part I: Question and Response (Items 1 to 10)<br>Directions: Choose the best response to each question or statement.","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Q:</b> My laptop just crashed, and I lost my essay!</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>R:</b> {{2}}</span>"],"qr3":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part I: Question and Response (Items 1 to 10)<br>Directions: Choose the best response to each question or statement.","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Q:</b> How many people are coming to the party?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>R:</b> {{3}}</span>"],"qr4":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part I: Question and Response (Items 1 to 10)<br>Directions: Choose the best response to each question or statement.","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Q:</b> Is this the right bus for the airport?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>R:</b> {{4}}</span>"],"qr5":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part I: Question and Response (Items 1 to 10)<br>Directions: Choose the best response to each question or statement.","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Q:</b> I'm so sorry for your loss.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>R:</b> {{5}}</span>"],"qr6":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part I: Question and Response (Items 1 to 10)<br>Directions: Choose the best response to each question or statement.","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Q:</b> Could you do me a favor?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>R:</b> {{6}}</span>"],"qr7":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part I: Question and Response (Items 1 to 10)<br>Directions: Choose the best response to each question or statement.","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Q:</b> How about going for a swim this afternoon?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>R:</b> {{7}}</span>"],"qr8":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part I: Question and Response (Items 1 to 10)<br>Directions: Choose the best response to each question or statement.","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Q:</b> Who's going to pick up the cake?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>R:</b> {{8}}</span>"],"qr9":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part I: Question and Response (Items 1 to 10)<br>Directions: Choose the best response to each question or statement.","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Q:</b> I can't stand this hot weather.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>R:</b> {{9}}</span>"],"qr10":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part I: Question and Response (Items 1 to 10)<br>Directions: Choose the best response to each question or statement.","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Q:</b> Did you remember to lock the door?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>R:</b> {{10}}</span>"],"c1":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part II: Short Conversations<br>Conversation 1 (Items 11 to 13)<br>Situation: At a bakery","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Customer:</b> Hi. {{11}}.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Staff:</b> Sure. What flavor would you like?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Customer:</b> {{12}}.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Staff:</b> Great choice. Would you like us to write a message on it?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Customer:</b> {{13}}.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Staff:</b> No problem. It'll be ready by noon on Saturday.</span>"],"c2":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part II: Short Conversations<br>Conversation 2 (Items 14 to 16)<br>Situation: Two friends talking on the phone outside a cinema","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Mook:</b> Where are you? The movie starts in ten minutes!</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Boss:</b> {{14}}.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Mook:</b> {{15}}?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Boss:</b> Yes, go ahead. I'll find you inside.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Mook:</b> {{16}}.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Boss:</b> Thanks. Sorry again for being late.</span>"],"c3":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part II: Short Conversations<br>Conversation 3 (Items 17 to 20)<br>Situation: At a hotel front desk in the morning","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Guest:</b> Good morning. {{17}}.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Staff:</b> Of course. May I have your room key, please?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Guest:</b> {{18}}.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Staff:</b> Thank you. Did you take anything from the minibar?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Guest:</b> {{19}}.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Staff:</b> That's 30 baht. How would you like to pay?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Guest:</b> {{20}}.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Staff:</b> Thank you. Have a safe trip home.</span>"],"c4":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part III: Long Conversations<br>Conversation 1 (Items 21 to 25)<br>Situation: A job interview at a cafe","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Manager:</b> Thanks for coming in, Tee. {{21}}?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Tee:</b> Yes, I worked at a coffee shop near my university for about a year.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Manager:</b> {{22}}?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Tee:</b> I liked talking with customers and remembering their favorite drinks.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Manager:</b> That's great. {{23}}?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Tee:</b> Yes, Saturdays and Sundays are fine, but I have classes on Monday mornings.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Manager:</b> {{24}}.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Tee:</b> Perfect. When could I start?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Manager:</b> {{25}}?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Tee:</b> That sounds great. Thank you so much!</span>"],"c5":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part III: Long Conversations<br>Conversation 2 (Items 26 to 30)<br>Situation: Two friends talking about recycling","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Ann:</b> {{26}}?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Bow:</b> I try to, but I'm never sure what can be recycled.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Ann:</b> {{27}}.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Bow:</b> What about food boxes?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Ann:</b> {{28}}.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Bow:</b> Oh, I didn't know that. I always throw them away with the rest.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Ann:</b> {{29}}.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Bow:</b> Is there a recycling center near here?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Ann:</b> {{30}}.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Bow:</b> Great. I'll take my bottles there this weekend.</span>"],"r1":["SECTION II: READING SKILL (Items 31 to 60)<br>Part I: Text Completion<br>Passage 1 (Items 31 to 35)<br>Directions: Choose the best answer to complete the passage.","<span class=\"xp\">Pad Thai is {{31}} the most famous Thai dish outside Thailand. Interestingly, it is a fairly recent creation. The dish became popular in the middle of the twentieth century, when the government promoted it {{32}} a period of rice shortage.</span><span class=\"xp\">Eating rice noodles was encouraged as a way {{33}} the amount of rice people consumed, and the dish soon spread across the country.</span><span class=\"xp\">Today, Pad Thai can be found in Thai restaurants all over the world, {{34}} it is often changed to suit local tastes. Some cooks abroad even {{35}} tamarind with tomato sauce, which gives the dish a very different flavor.</span>"],"r2":["SECTION II: READING SKILL (Items 31 to 60)<br>Part I: Text Completion<br>Passage 2 (Items 36 to 40)<br>Directions: Choose the best answer to complete the passage.","<span class=\"xp\">Mental health is {{36}} important as physical health, yet many university students do not seek help {{37}} they are struggling with stress or anxiety.</span><span class=\"xp\">Many universities now offer free counseling services, {{38}} students can use privately. Experts also encourage students {{39}} to someone they trust, such as a friend or family member, when they feel overwhelmed.</span><span class=\"xp\">In addition, small daily habits, such as getting enough sleep and exercising regularly, can make a big {{40}} to how students feel.</span>"],"r3":["SECTION II: READING SKILL (Items 31 to 60)<br>Part I: Text Completion<br>Passage 3 (Items 41 to 45)<br>Directions: Choose the best answer to complete the passage.","<span class=\"xp\">Online shopping has {{41}} easier than ever to buy almost anything with just a few clicks. However, it has also {{42}} a huge increase in packaging waste and delivery traffic.</span><span class=\"xp\">Some customers order several sizes of the same item and send back the ones that do not fit, a habit {{43}} “bracketing.” This creates extra transport and often means that returned items cannot be sold again.</span><span class=\"xp\">Some companies now charge a small fee for returns, {{44}} to discourage this habit. Others offer online tools that help customers choose the right size {{45}} they place an order.</span>"],"p1":["SECTION II: READING SKILL (Items 31 to 60)<br>Part II: Reading Comprehension<br>Passage 1 (Items 46 to 50)<br>Directions: Read the passage and choose the best answer to each question.","<span class=\"xp\">(1) When Kwan told her family that she wanted to become a car mechanic, her relatives laughed. “That’s a job for men,” one of them said. But Kwan had loved fixing things since she was a child, often taking apart old radios and putting them back together.</span><span class=\"xp\">(2) After finishing vocational college, she applied to more than ten garages, but most of them rejected her without even giving her a test. Finally, a small garage in Lampang agreed to let her work for a trial month.</span><span class=\"xp\">(3) Kwan worked harder than anyone else. She arrived early, stayed late, and asked the senior mechanics endless questions. By the end of the month, the owner offered her a full time position. Soon, several customers began asking for her by name because she explained problems clearly and honestly.</span><span class=\"xp\">(4) Five years later, Kwan runs her own garage and trains young women who want to enter the field. “Skills don’t have a gender,” she says. “Anyone who is willing to learn can do this job.”</span>"],"p2":["SECTION II: READING SKILL (Items 31 to 60)<br>Part II: Reading Comprehension<br>Passage 2 (Items 51 to 55)<br>Directions: Read the passage and choose the best answer to each question.","<span class=\"xp\">(1) Lotus plants grow in muddy ponds, yet their leaves always seem perfectly clean. This puzzled scientists for many years until they examined the leaves under powerful microscopes.</span><span class=\"xp\">(2) They discovered that the surface of a lotus leaf is covered with tiny bumps, which are themselves coated with a waxy material. Because of this structure, water cannot spread across the leaf. Instead, it forms round drops that roll off easily.</span><span class=\"xp\">(3) As the drops roll, they pick up dirt and dust and carry them away. This self cleaning ability, known as the lotus effect, helps the plant stay healthy by keeping its leaves free of dirt that could block sunlight.</span><span class=\"xp\">(4) Engineers have since copied this idea to create products such as glass that cleans itself when it rains, fabrics that stay dry in wet weather, and paints that help keep buildings clean. The lotus is a good example of how nature can inspire human inventions.</span>"],"p3":["SECTION II: READING SKILL (Items 31 to 60)<br>Part II: Reading Comprehension<br>Passage 3 (Items 56 to 60)<br>Directions: Read the passage and choose the best answer to each question.","<span class=\"xp\">(1) Zoos have long been popular places for families to spend a weekend. In recent years, however, animal rights groups have questioned whether keeping wild animals in captivity can ever be justified.</span><span class=\"xp\">(2) Supporters say that modern zoos play an important role in conservation. Many run breeding programs for endangered species and even return some animals to the wild. Zoos also educate millions of visitors, especially children, about wildlife and the threats it faces.</span><span class=\"xp\">(3) Critics argue that even the best zoos cannot provide the space and freedom that wild animals need. Large animals such as elephants and tigers may show signs of stress, such as walking back and forth for hours, when they are kept in small enclosures. Critics believe the money should instead be used to protect animals in their natural habitats.</span><span class=\"xp\">(4) Some zoos are responding by keeping fewer species, building larger and more natural enclosures, and working closely with wildlife sanctuaries. Whether such changes are enough, however, remains an open question.</span>"]},"TG70_I":"","TG70_P":{"qr1":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part I: Question and Response (Items 1 to 10)<br>Directions: Choose the best response to each question or statement.","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Q:</b> Would you mind keeping an eye on my bag while I grab a coffee?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>R:</b> {{1}}</span>"],"qr2":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part I: Question and Response (Items 1 to 10)<br>Directions: Choose the best response to each question or statement.","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Q:</b> I can't believe I failed my driving test again.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>R:</b> {{2}}</span>"],"qr3":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part I: Question and Response (Items 1 to 10)<br>Directions: Choose the best response to each question or statement.","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Q:</b> How long does it take to get to the airport from here?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>R:</b> {{3}}</span>"],"qr4":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part I: Question and Response (Items 1 to 10)<br>Directions: Choose the best response to each question or statement.","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Q:</b> Do you happen to know if the library is open on Sundays?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>R:</b> {{4}}</span>"],"qr5":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part I: Question and Response (Items 1 to 10)<br>Directions: Choose the best response to each question or statement.","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Q:</b> I'm afraid I spilled coffee on your notebook. I'm so sorry.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>R:</b> {{5}}</span>"],"qr6":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part I: Question and Response (Items 1 to 10)<br>Directions: Choose the best response to each question or statement.","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Q:</b> Shall we split the bill?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>R:</b> {{6}}</span>"],"qr7":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part I: Question and Response (Items 1 to 10)<br>Directions: Choose the best response to each question or statement.","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Q:</b> Why don't you take a day off? You look exhausted.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>R:</b> {{7}}</span>"],"qr8":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part I: Question and Response (Items 1 to 10)<br>Directions: Choose the best response to each question or statement.","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Q:</b> Is it okay if I bring a friend to your birthday party?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>R:</b> {{8}}</span>"],"qr9":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part I: Question and Response (Items 1 to 10)<br>Directions: Choose the best response to each question or statement.","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Q:</b> What do you think of the new canteen menu?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>R:</b> {{9}}</span>"],"qr10":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part I: Question and Response (Items 1 to 10)<br>Directions: Choose the best response to each question or statement.","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Q:</b> Guess what? I've been offered a scholarship to study in Japan!</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>R:</b> {{10}}</span>"],"c1":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part II: Short Conversations<br>Conversation 1 (Items 11 to 13)<br>Situation: At a pharmacy","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Pharmacist:</b> Good evening. {{11}}?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Customer:</b> Yes, please. I've had a sore throat and a runny nose since yesterday.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Pharmacist:</b> I see. {{12}}?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Customer:</b> No, I'm not. I've taken this kind of medicine before without any problems.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Pharmacist:</b> Good. Then take one tablet three times a day after meals, and drink plenty of water.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Customer:</b> {{13}}?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Pharmacist:</b> About five days. If you don't feel better by then, please see a doctor.</span>"],"c2":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part II: Short Conversations<br>Conversation 2 (Items 14 to 16)<br>Situation: Two classmates talking about a group project","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Mint:</b> Have you finished your part of the slides yet?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Ben:</b> Not yet. {{14}}.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Mint:</b> Really? But the presentation is this Friday!</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Ben:</b> I know. My laptop broke down last night, and I lost half of my work.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Mint:</b> Oh no. {{15}}?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Ben:</b> That would be great. Could I borrow it this evening?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Mint:</b> Sure. {{16}}.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Ben:</b> Don't worry. I've learned my lesson.</span>"],"c3":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part II: Short Conversations<br>Conversation 3 (Items 17 to 20)<br>Situation: At a hotel front desk","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Guest:</b> Hi, I'd like to check in, please. The reservation is under Arisa Wong.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Receptionist:</b> Welcome to the Riverside Hotel. {{17}}</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Guest:</b> Here you are.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Receptionist:</b> Thank you. You're in room 512 on the fifth floor. Breakfast is served from 6:30 to 10:00 in the restaurant on the ground floor.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Guest:</b> Great. {{18}}?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Receptionist:</b> Yes, there is, and it is free. The password is printed on your key card holder.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Guest:</b> Perfect. Oh, and {{19}}?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Receptionist:</b> Certainly. We can keep your bags at the front desk until 6 p.m. on the day you leave.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Guest:</b> {{20}}</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Receptionist:</b> You're welcome. Enjoy your stay.</span>"],"c4":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part III: Long Conversations<br>Conversation 1 (Items 21 to 25)<br>Situation: A university student meets an academic advisor","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Advisor:</b> Come in, Nat. {{21}}?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Nat:</b> Thanks for seeing me. I'm thinking about dropping my statistics course. I'm completely lost in class.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Advisor:</b> I see. Before you make a decision, {{22}}.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Nat:</b> Well, I failed the first quiz, and I don't understand most of the lectures.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Advisor:</b> Have you tried going to the tutoring center? Many students find it really useful.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Nat:</b> Not yet. To be honest, {{23}}.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Advisor:</b> There's nothing to be embarrassed about. The tutors are senior students who once struggled with the same course.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Nat:</b> Really? Then maybe I should give it a try.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Advisor:</b> I think so. Also, if you drop the course now, {{24}}.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Nat:</b> Oh, I didn't know that. I'd better not drop it, then.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Advisor:</b> Good decision. Come back in three weeks and {{25}}.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Nat:</b> I will. Thank you so much.</span>"],"c5":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part III: Long Conversations<br>Conversation 2 (Items 26 to 30)<br>Situation: A customer calls an online shop","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Agent:</b> Thank you for calling ShopSmart. This is Jenny speaking. {{26}}?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Customer:</b> Hi. I ordered a pair of running shoes last week, but the ones I received are the wrong size.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Agent:</b> I'm sorry about that. {{27}}?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Customer:</b> Sure. It's TH478152.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Agent:</b> Thank you. I can see that you ordered size 42, but we sent you size 40. That was our mistake.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Customer:</b> {{28}}?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Agent:</b> Of course. We'll send a courier to collect the wrong pair tomorrow, and the new pair will arrive within three days.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Customer:</b> That's great. {{29}}?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Agent:</b> No, you won't. The return is completely free of charge.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Customer:</b> Wonderful. {{30}}</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Agent:</b> My pleasure. Have a nice day.</span>"],"r1":["SECTION II: READING SKILL (Items 31 to 60)<br>Part I: Text Completion<br>Passage 1 (Items 31 to 35)<br>Directions: Choose the best answer to complete the passage.","<span class=\"xp\">Every year between January and April, many provinces in northern Thailand are covered by a thick haze. The main source of this pollution is {{31}} burning, as farmers clear their fields quickly and cheaply after the harvest. Forest fires and traffic emissions also {{32}} to the problem.</span><span class=\"xp\">When the level of fine dust, known as PM2.5, rises above the safe limit, schools may close and hospitals report more patients with breathing problems. {{33}}, tourism in cities such as Chiang Mai suffers, since visitors are less willing to travel to places with poor air quality.</span><span class=\"xp\">Experts say that the problem cannot be solved by {{34}} alone. Farmers need affordable alternatives, such as machines that turn crop waste into animal feed or fertilizer. Until such solutions become widely available, residents are advised to check the air quality index daily and to wear masks {{35}} they go outdoors.</span>"],"r2":["SECTION II: READING SKILL (Items 31 to 60)<br>Part I: Text Completion<br>Passage 2 (Items 36 to 40)<br>Directions: Choose the best answer to complete the passage.","<span class=\"xp\">In an age when almost any information can be found online, many people assume that public libraries are {{36}}. Yet libraries around the world are proving them wrong by reinventing themselves as community centers.</span><span class=\"xp\">Instead of simply lending books, modern libraries now offer free coding classes, workshops with 3D printers, and quiet rooms {{37}} students can study late into the night. In Helsinki, the Oodi Central Library, {{38}} opened in 2018, attracts thousands of visitors a day and has become one of the most popular places in the city.</span><span class=\"xp\">Librarians say that their role has changed {{39}} gatekeepers of knowledge to guides who help people find, evaluate, and use information wisely. This skill is more important than ever in a world full of fake news. Far from disappearing, libraries may be more {{40}} today than at any time in history.</span>"],"r3":["SECTION II: READING SKILL (Items 31 to 60)<br>Part I: Text Completion<br>Passage 3 (Items 41 to 45)<br>Directions: Choose the best answer to complete the passage.","<span class=\"xp\">Each year, roughly one third of all food produced for people is lost or wasted. This waste not only {{41}} families and businesses billions of dollars but also harms the environment, because rotting food in landfills releases methane, a greenhouse gas far more powerful than carbon dioxide.</span><span class=\"xp\">In Thailand, food makes up a large share of household waste, {{42}} much of it is still perfectly safe to eat when it is thrown away. Some supermarkets have begun selling “ugly” fruit and vegetables at lower prices, while mobile apps now connect restaurants with customers who want to buy leftover meals at a discount.</span><span class=\"xp\">Individuals can also make a difference. Planning meals before shopping, storing food properly, and understanding the difference between “best before” and “use by” dates can {{43}} reduce the amount we throw away. If every household cut its food waste {{44}} half, the impact on both family budgets and the planet would be enormous. In the end, tackling food waste is less about sacrifice and more about {{45}} what we already have.</span>"],"p1":["SECTION II: READING SKILL (Items 31 to 60)<br>Part II: Reading Comprehension<br>Passage 1 (Items 46 to 50)<br>Directions: Read the passage and choose the best answer to each question.","<span class=\"xp\">(1) When Ploy graduated with a degree in marketing, her friends expected her to take a job with a good salary in Bangkok. Instead, she returned to her home village in Roi Et, where her grandmother and neighbors had been weaving <i>pha khao ma</i>, a traditional checked cloth, for decades. Ploy had always loved the cloth, but she noticed that the weavers earned very little because they sold their products to middlemen at low prices.</span><span class=\"xp\">(2) Ploy decided to change that. She photographed the weavers at work, wrote short stories about the meaning of each pattern, and began selling the cloth online. At first, orders were few, and some villagers doubted that young people in the city would ever pay for something they saw as out of date.</span><span class=\"xp\">(3) Everything changed when a popular travel blogger wore one of Ploy’s scarves in a video that went viral. Within a month, Ploy received more orders than the village could produce. Rather than buying cheaper cloth from factories to meet the demand, she chose to train younger villagers to weave, paying them fairly for every piece.</span><span class=\"xp\">(4) Today, more than forty families earn a steady income from the business. “I didn’t want to just sell a product,” Ploy says. “I wanted the people who make it to be proud of it again.”</span>"],"p2":["SECTION II: READING SKILL (Items 31 to 60)<br>Part II: Reading Comprehension<br>Passage 2 (Items 51 to 55)<br>Directions: Read the passage and choose the best answer to each question.","<span class=\"xp\">(1) On a hot afternoon, the center of Bangkok can be several degrees warmer than the rice fields just outside the city. Scientists call this the urban heat island effect. It occurs because concrete, asphalt, and glass absorb heat during the day and release it slowly at night, while cars, air conditioners, and factories add even more heat to the air.</span><span class=\"xp\">(2) The effect is more than just uncomfortable. Higher temperatures increase the demand for electricity as people turn up their air conditioners, which in turn pump more hot air into the streets. This vicious circle also raises health risks, particularly for the elderly, outdoor workers, and people living in crowded housing without proper ventilation.</span><span class=\"xp\">(3) Fortunately, cities are fighting back. Singapore requires many new buildings to replace the greenery lost during construction with rooftop gardens and plant walls. Other cities paint roofs white to reflect sunlight, and some replace dark pavements with materials that stay cooler. In Bangkok, Chulalongkorn University Centenary Park was designed not only to provide shade and green space but also to collect rainwater and reduce flooding.</span><span class=\"xp\">(4) Urban planners admit that no single solution will be enough. However, they agree that every tree planted and every roof turned green brings the city one step closer to a cooler, healthier future.</span>"],"p3":["SECTION II: READING SKILL (Items 31 to 60)<br>Part II: Reading Comprehension<br>Passage 3 (Items 56 to 60)<br>Directions: Read the passage and choose the best answer to each question.","<span class=\"xp\">(1) When AI chatbots that can write essays in seconds became widely available, many schools reacted with alarm. Some banned the tools completely, fearing that students would stop thinking for themselves. A few years later, however, the debate has become far more nuanced.</span><span class=\"xp\">(2) Supporters argue that AI, used properly, can act as a patient tutor available at any hour. A student who is confused about a math problem at midnight can ask for an explanation broken into small steps. Teachers, too, can save time by using AI to prepare practice questions, giving them more time to work with students individually.</span><span class=\"xp\">(3) Critics, on the other hand, point out that chatbots sometimes produce information that sounds convincing but is completely false. Students who copy answers without checking them may not only learn incorrect facts but also lose the chance to develop their own writing and reasoning skills. “Struggling with a difficult problem is part of learning,” says one high school teacher. “If the machine does the struggling, the student doesn’t grow.”</span><span class=\"xp\">(4) Many educators now believe that the real question is not whether students should use AI, but how. Some schools ask students to submit the questions they asked a chatbot along with their final work, so that teachers can see their thinking process. Others design assignments, such as oral presentations and classroom debates, that AI cannot easily complete. In the end, the goal is the same as it has always been: to produce learners who can think critically, with or without a machine.</span>"]}};
